/* Original practice tests (Sessions 19-22). Loaded on demand. */
(function(){"use strict";window.estTests=Object.assign(window.estTests||{},{"s20-practice-test-1":{"session":20,"title":"English Practice Test 1","intro":"A condensed 30-question practice test plus 12 drill questions. It is not a full 85-item EST simulation. Suggested timing: 32 minutes for Questions 1–30 (about 11 minutes per module), then 12–15 minutes for the drills.","perModule":null,"modules":[{"name":"Module 1: Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage A: Measuring Street Shade","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"In many cities, summer heat does not affect every street equally. Wide roads and parking lots absorb sunlight, while tree-lined streets provide shade and release moisture through their leaves. In 2023, the city of Bellford began a pilot project along a busy bus corridor where residents had reported uncomfortable afternoon temperatures. The project added native trees at twelve locations and installed small sensors to record air and pavement temperatures."},{"t":"p","x":"During the first summer, pavement beneath mature trees was often 8 to 11 degrees Celsius cooler than pavement in nearby unshaded areas. The readings did not prove that trees alone caused every difference: building height, wind, and the amount of traffic also influenced local conditions. Still, the measurements gave planners a useful starting point. They could compare the same locations over time rather than relying only on residents’ impressions."},{"t":"p","x":"The project also changed how the city prepared planting sites. Earlier efforts had placed young trees in small openings surrounded by compacted soil. Many of those trees struggled because their roots could not reach enough water or oxygen. Bellford’s new tree pits used permeable surfaces and larger soil areas. Volunteers watered the trees during the first two summers, and city staff recorded survival rates each autumn."},{"t":"p","x":"After three years, 87 percent of the pilot trees remained healthy, compared with 63 percent in several earlier planting projects. The comparison is encouraging, but it is not a controlled experiment: the newer trees received more maintenance, and the sites differed in age and exposure. The city therefore plans to continue collecting data before expanding the program. Its next report will consider not only temperature readings but also maintenance costs, pedestrian use, and residents’ access to shaded bus stops."},{"t":"p","x":"For Bellford, the pilot’s main value may be its combination of practical work and careful measurement. Planting trees can make a street more comfortable, but tracking the results helps planners decide where future investments are most likely to help."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"q":"In Passage A, which revision is the most concise and precise replacement for “gave planners a useful starting point” in the second paragraph?","c":["NO CHANGE","provided planners with a useful place from which to begin their work","offered planners an initial baseline for comparison","gave planners something useful that they could start with"],"a":2,"why":"“Initial baseline for comparison” states the function of the measurements directly and removes unnecessary wording."},{"n":2,"q":"Which option correctly punctuates the list in this sentence from Passage A: “The next report will consider temperature readings maintenance costs pedestrian use and residents’ access to shaded bus stops”?","c":["readings, maintenance costs, pedestrian use, and residents’ access","readings; maintenance costs; pedestrian use; and residents’ access","readings: maintenance costs, pedestrian use, and residents’ access","readings maintenance costs; pedestrian use and residents’ access"],"a":0,"why":"Commas separate the items in a straightforward series, with a comma before and."},{"n":3,"q":"In Passage A, which word best completes the sentence: “The readings did not ___ that trees alone caused every difference”?","c":["effect","affect","prove","conclude"],"a":2,"why":"“Prove” fits the meaning: the readings did not establish the cause with certainty."},{"n":4,"q":"Which transition best connects the encouraging survival comparison to the limitation that follows? “The comparison is encouraging. ___, the study is not a controlled experiment.”","c":["For example","Nevertheless","Similarly","In particular"],"a":1,"why":"The second sentence qualifies the encouraging comparison; nevertheless signals that limitation."},{"n":5,"q":"Which sentence best supports the main focus of Passage A?","c":["Bellford has several busy roads that carry buses throughout the day.","The pilot combines tree planting with measurement to guide future decisions.","Mature trees usually have larger roots than young trees.","Volunteers prefer planting native trees to planting imported species."],"a":1,"why":"The passage repeatedly links practical planting with data collection and cautious planning."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage B: A Library That Lends Tools","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A small public library in Easton recently introduced a tool-lending shelf. Alongside books, members can borrow hand drills, sewing machines, basic gardening equipment, and repair kits for up to one week. The idea came from a neighborhood survey in which many residents said they owned items they used only once or twice a year. Some people wanted to repair a loose cabinet hinge or plant a few herbs, but they did not want to buy equipment that would spend most of its life in storage."},{"t":"p","x":"The library did not assume that lending tools would automatically reduce waste. Before opening the shelf, staff met with a local repair group to learn which tools were safe for beginners and which required training. Each item received a numbered label, a short instruction card, and a checklist for staff to use when it returned. Borrowers could also attend a monthly demonstration where volunteers explained safe use and basic maintenance."},{"t":"p","x":"In the first six months, the shelf recorded 214 loans. Hand drills and garden tools circulated most often, while the sewing machines were borrowed less frequently but usually for longer periods. Staff interviewed a sample of borrowers. Several reported completing small repairs they had postponed, and a few said they had shared newly learned skills with family members. These comments suggest that the shelf may help residents build confidence as well as access equipment."},{"t":"p","x":"The figures have limits. A loan does not prove that a purchase was avoided, and the interviews represent only a small part of the membership. The library also spends time checking equipment and replacing worn parts. To evaluate the program fairly, staff plan to compare maintenance costs with use rates and to ask borrowers whether they would otherwise have purchased or rented the same item."},{"t":"p","x":"The tool shelf is not meant to replace hardware stores or professional repair services. Instead, it offers another option for occasional tasks and creates a place where neighbors can exchange knowledge. If the next year’s records show steady use and manageable costs, the library may add equipment requested by members."}]},"questions":[{"n":6,"q":"In Passage B, which relative pronoun correctly completes the sentence: “The repair group, ___ trained the volunteers, helped the library select safe equipment”?","c":["whose","whom","which","who"],"a":3,"why":"The pronoun is the subject of “trained” and refers to people, so who is correct."},{"n":7,"q":"Which revision best corrects the verb tense in Passage B if the writer wants to describe the library’s continuing procedure? “Each item received a numbered label, a short instruction card, and a checklist for staff to use when it returned.”","c":["NO CHANGE","Each item receives a numbered label, a short instruction card, and a checklist for staff to use when it returns.","Each item had received a numbered label, a short instruction card, and a checklist for staff to use when it returned.","Each item receiving a numbered label, a short instruction card, and a checklist for staff to use when it returned."],"a":1,"why":"The present tense consistently describes a current procedure."},{"n":8,"q":"Which sentence best combines the ideas without creating a comma splice? “A loan does not prove that a purchase was avoided. The interviews represent only a small part of the membership.”","c":["A loan does not prove that a purchase was avoided, the interviews represent only a small part of the membership.","A loan does not prove that a purchase was avoided; the interviews represent only a small part of the membership.","A loan does not prove that a purchase was avoided, and because the interviews represent only a small part of the membership.","A loan does not prove that a purchase was avoided; and, the interviews represent only a small part of the membership."],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon correctly joins two closely related independent clauses."},{"n":9,"q":"Which choice best states the main conclusion of Passage B?","c":["Libraries should stop lending books and focus on tools.","Tool-lending programs can offer useful access, but their effects and costs need careful evaluation.","Borrowers always avoid buying tools when libraries lend them.","Professional repair services are unnecessary when communities share equipment."],"a":1,"why":"The author presents benefits and limits, then recommends continued evaluation."},{"n":10,"q":"Which revision makes the list parallel? “The library plans to add requested tools, improving its return checklist, and to train more volunteers.”","c":["NO CHANGE","adding requested tools, improving its return checklist, and training more volunteers","to add requested tools, improve its return checklist, and train more volunteers","to add requested tools, improving its return checklist, and training more volunteers"],"a":2,"why":"The three actions follow the same infinitive pattern: to add, improve, and train."}]}]},{"name":"Module 2: Reading","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage A: A Trial In The Graywater Estuary","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"For decades, a stretch of the Graywater estuary was treated mainly as unused land. A road embankment and drainage channels had cut parts of the marsh off from the daily movement of the tide. Over time, reeds thinned in several sections, and muddy banks eroded during storms. Local officials initially considered reinforcing the banks with a continuous line of concrete barriers. A university ecologist proposed a different approach: restore tidal movement in selected areas and allow marsh plants to recolonize the shallow ground."},{"t":"p","x":"The restoration began with a small test site rather than the entire estuary. Engineers opened two narrow channels and removed debris that blocked the flow of water. Volunteers planted native marsh grasses in places where seedlings were unlikely to establish on their own. The team also marked permanent measurement points to track water depth, sediment movement, plant cover, and the number of juvenile fish observed during monthly surveys."},{"t":"p","x":"The first year produced mixed results. Some seedlings failed during an unusually dry period, and one channel collected more sediment than expected. Yet the monitoring records showed that water reached areas that had previously remained dry at high tide. By the third year, plant cover had increased at most measurement points, and the team recorded more juvenile fish in the restored channels than in the nearby untreated section. The researchers were careful not to claim that the restoration alone caused every change. Rainfall, water temperature, and seasonal variation also affect fish populations."},{"t":"p","x":"The marsh may provide benefits beyond habitat. Dense vegetation can slow water moving across the shore, while roots help hold sediment in place. These effects may reduce erosion during some storms, although the project has not yet experienced a storm large enough to test its limits. The team is therefore comparing the restored site with other sections of the estuary and keeping records through different seasons."},{"t":"p","x":"The project has changed the public discussion as well. Residents who once saw the marsh as neglected land now attend monitoring days and ask how the channels work. Officials have postponed a decision about concrete barriers until the next evaluation. The experiment does not show that engineered protection is never needed. It shows that restoring natural processes can be tested, measured, and compared with more rigid alternatives before a costly decision becomes permanent."}]},"questions":[{"n":11,"q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of the Graywater estuary passage?","c":["Concrete barriers always protect shorelines more effectively than marshes.","A measured restoration trial is helping officials evaluate marsh recovery before choosing a permanent shoreline strategy.","The estuary’s fish population has returned completely to its original level.","Residents opposed all scientific monitoring of the marsh."],"a":1,"why":"The passage describes a trial, measured results, limitations, and a postponed decision about permanent protection."},{"n":12,"q":"What can reasonably be inferred about the team’s decision to begin with a small test site?","c":["The team lacked any method for measuring changes across the estuary.","A limited trial allowed the team to observe results before expanding the intervention.","The team knew that the full estuary would recover within one year.","Officials had already rejected every engineered option."],"a":1,"why":"The text says the restoration began with a small site and that officials delayed a larger decision pending evaluation."},{"n":13,"q":"As used in the passage, “recolonize” most nearly means:","c":["return to and establish a presence in an area","compete with every existing species","remove sediment from a channel","migrate permanently away from a habitat"],"a":0,"why":"The marsh plants are returning to shallow ground where they had become less common."},{"n":14,"q":"Why does the author mention that one channel collected more sediment than expected?","c":["To prove that the project failed completely","To show that the team ignored its monitoring records","To illustrate that the early results were mixed and required continued observation","To argue that sediment has no role in marsh restoration"],"a":2,"why":"The detail qualifies the positive findings and shows why the team continued monitoring."},{"n":15,"q":"Which detail provides the strongest support for the claim that the restored channels changed local conditions?","c":["Residents now attend monitoring days.","Officials considered concrete barriers.","Water reached areas that had previously remained dry at high tide.","The project began with a small test site."],"a":2,"why":"This is a direct observation of a physical change at the site."},{"n":16,"q":"Which best describes the author’s tone?","c":["Cautiously analytical","Bitter and accusatory","Celebratory and certain","Humorous and dismissive"],"a":0,"why":"The author reports encouraging findings while emphasizing limits and the need for further testing."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage B: A Network Of Local Weather Stations","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A citywide network of low-cost weather stations can reveal patterns that a single airport station may miss. An airport instrument provides a consistent long-term record, but it cannot describe every neighborhood’s conditions. Hills, buildings, tree cover, and distance from the sea can produce small-scale differences in rainfall and temperature. A network of local stations can help researchers map those differences, provided that the instruments are checked and their limitations are made clear."},{"t":"p","x":"In Northbridge, a university team worked with schools and community centers to install twelve rain gauges. Volunteers read the gauges at the same time each morning and entered the results into a shared database. Before the project began, the team trained volunteers to place the gauges away from roofs, trees, and walls that might distort the amount of rain collected. Researchers periodically compared the readings with a reference instrument and flagged measurements that appeared inconsistent. When a volunteer missed a reading, the database marked it as missing rather than substituting an estimated value. That choice left gaps in the record but prevented guesses from appearing as observations."},{"t":"p","x":"The first wet-season report showed that rainfall varied across the city on several storm days. During one storm, gauges near the eastern hills recorded more rain than gauges closer to the harbor. The difference matched the team’s expectation that rising air near the hills could cool and release moisture. Still, the researchers warned against treating a single storm as proof of a permanent pattern. Wind direction, storm movement, and gauge placement can all affect a reading."},{"t":"p","x":"The network’s value lies partly in the questions it allows residents to ask. A school might compare rainfall near its garden with readings from a nearby park. Emergency planners might examine whether heavy rain repeatedly affects the same low-lying streets. Researchers can also identify gaps in the data and decide where another gauge would be most useful. The network does not replace professional forecasting; it adds local detail that a broad regional measurement may overlook."},{"t":"p","x":"The team publishes the raw readings alongside notes about missing data and instrument checks. That transparency matters because a map can look precise even when some measurements are uncertain. The researchers hope that residents will use the records as evidence for questions, not as a shortcut to certainty. A reliable local network depends on repeated observations, careful placement, and a willingness to revise early conclusions when new data appear."},{"t":"h","x":"Rainfall recorded during one storm:"},{"t":"table","x":{"head":["Site","Rainfall"],"rows":[["Eastern Hills","31 mm"],["Central School","22 mm"],["Harbor Park","18 mm"]]}}]},"questions":[{"n":17,"q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of the Northbridge passage?","c":["Community weather stations can add useful local detail when volunteers follow careful procedures and researchers acknowledge uncertainty.","Airport stations should be closed because they cannot measure rainfall accurately.","Rainfall always increases near hills, regardless of weather conditions.","Volunteers can replace professional meteorologists after brief training."],"a":0,"why":"The passage explains the value of local data while stressing calibration, placement, and limitations."},{"n":18,"q":"Why did the team train volunteers to place gauges away from roofs, trees, and walls?","c":["To make the gauges less visible to the public","To reduce the chance that nearby objects would distort the collected rainfall","To ensure that every gauge recorded identical amounts","To prevent volunteers from comparing results"],"a":1,"why":"The passage explicitly states that those objects might distort the amount collected."},{"n":19,"q":"As used in the passage, “transparent” most nearly means:","c":["made of glass","easy to ignore","open about methods and limitations","certain to be correct"],"a":2,"why":"Publishing raw readings and notes about missing data makes the process open to scrutiny."},{"n":20,"q":"The table shows rainfall during one storm: Eastern Hills 31 mm; Central School 22 mm; Harbor Park 18 mm. Which conclusion is best supported by the table and passage?","c":["The eastern hills received more recorded rain than the other two sites during this storm.","The harbor receives more rain than the hills in every season.","The school gauge must have been broken.","The data prove that hills cause all local rainfall differences."],"a":0,"why":"The table supports a comparison for this storm only, not a permanent or universal claim."},{"n":21,"q":"Which practice most directly helps the researchers judge whether a station’s readings are reliable?","c":["Publishing only the highest rainfall value","Comparing readings with a reference instrument and flagging inconsistencies","Asking residents to guess how much rain fell","Removing all data from days with heavy rainfall"],"a":1,"why":"The passage identifies comparison with a reference instrument and checks for inconsistent readings as reliability procedures."},{"n":22,"q":"How does the final paragraph develop the author’s argument?","c":["It rejects the need for public data.","It explains why transparent records help users treat measurements as evidence rather than certainty.","It claims that every local reading is more accurate than an airport reading.","It shifts from rainfall to an unrelated discussion of city transport."],"a":1,"why":"The final paragraph connects data transparency with responsible interpretation and cautious conclusions."}]}]},{"name":"Module 3: Reading And Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":null,"questions":[{"n":23,"q":"Choose the most grammatically correct option. “A collection of field notes ___ stored in the archive.”","c":["are","were","is","have been"],"a":2,"why":"The subject is the singular noun collection; the prepositional phrase does not change its number."},{"n":24,"q":"Choose the transition that best completes the passage: “The first survey attracted few responses. ___, the researchers shortened the questionnaire and sent a reminder.”","c":["Similarly","As a result","For instance","In contrast"],"a":1,"why":"The second action responds to the low response rate, so a cause-and-result transition fits."},{"n":25,"q":"A short report explains that a school garden produced fewer vegetables during a dry month, but students still used it to study soil moisture and pollinators. What is the report’s main point?","c":["The garden failed and should be removed.","A garden can remain educationally useful even when its harvest is limited.","Pollinators prevent all crop losses.","Dry weather improves every school garden."],"a":1,"why":"The text contrasts a smaller harvest with continuing educational value."},{"n":26,"q":"Choose the sentence with correct punctuation.","c":["The team recorded three measurements, temperature, rainfall and wind speed.","The team recorded three measurements: temperature, rainfall, and wind speed.","The team recorded: three measurements temperature, rainfall, and wind speed.","The team recorded three measurements;: temperature, rainfall, and wind speed."],"a":1,"why":"A colon follows the complete clause and introduces the list."},{"n":27,"q":"Which revision is the most concise? “Due to the fact that the bridge was closed, the bus followed a different route.”","c":["Because the bridge was closed, the bus followed a different route.","Owing to the fact of the bridge’s closure, the bus followed a different route.","The bridge was closed, and this was the reason why the bus followed a different route.","Because of the fact that the bridge was closed, the bus followed a different route."],"a":0,"why":"Because expresses the cause directly and removes unnecessary wording."},{"n":28,"q":"Text 1 says a town’s evening market gives small producers a place to sell directly to customers. Text 2 says the market also hosts cooking demonstrations that attract families. Which statement best synthesizes the two texts?","c":["The market serves only professional chefs.","The market supports direct sales and offers activities that broaden its community appeal.","The cooking demonstrations have eliminated the need for local producers.","Both texts argue that markets should operate only in the evening."],"a":1,"why":"The synthesis combines the distinct but compatible points from both texts."},{"n":29,"q":"A writer says, “The first map was useful, but the second was indispensable because it included the missing river crossing.” What can be inferred?","c":["The first map contained no useful information.","The second map provided information essential to the task.","Neither map showed any rivers.","The writer disliked using maps."],"a":1,"why":"Indispensable means necessary; the missing crossing made the second map essential."},{"n":30,"q":"Choose the best completion. “The observatory, ___ roof was repaired last year, reopened to visitors.”","c":["who","whom","whose","where"],"a":2,"why":"Whose indicates possession: the roof belongs to the observatory."}]}]},{"name":"Drills","scored":false,"blocks":[{"passage":null,"questions":[{"n":31,"q":"Choose the correct sentence boundary.","c":["The data were incomplete, the team repeated the trial.","The data were incomplete; the team repeated the trial.","The data were incomplete; because the team repeated the trial.","The data were incomplete the team; repeated the trial."],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon joins two independent clauses; the other choices create a splice or an incomplete clause."},{"n":32,"q":"Choose the best transition. “The sample size was small. ___, the findings should be treated as preliminary.”","c":["Therefore","Meanwhile","For example","Likewise"],"a":0,"why":"The small sample size is the reason for treating the findings cautiously."},{"n":33,"q":"Which option uses the apostrophe correctly?","c":["The researchers notebook was missing.","The researchers' notebook was missing.","The researchers's notebook was missing.","The researcher’s' notebook was missing."],"a":1,"why":"The plural noun researchers takes an apostrophe after the final s to show plural possession."},{"n":34,"q":"Choose the most parallel version.","c":["The program teaches students to plan, collecting data, and how to report results.","The program teaches students planning, to collect data, and reporting results.","The program teaches students to plan, collect data, and report results.","The program teaches students to plan, data collection, and reporting results."],"a":2,"why":"All three coordinated actions use the infinitive pattern: to plan, collect, and report."},{"n":35,"q":"Choose the best correction. “After examining the chart, the conclusion seemed clear.”","c":["After examining the chart, the conclusion seemed clear.","After examining the chart, the analyst found the conclusion clear.","The conclusion, after examining the chart, seemed clear.","After the chart examined, the conclusion seemed clear."],"a":1,"why":"The analyst examines the chart; naming the analyst as the subject fixes the dangling modifier."},{"n":36,"q":"Which sentence correctly uses a nonessential clause?","c":["The observatory which opened in 1910, now offers tours.","The observatory, which opened in 1910, now offers tours.","The observatory, which opened in 1910 now offers tours.","The observatory which opened, in 1910 now offers tours."],"a":1,"why":"The nonessential clause needs a comma on both sides."},{"n":37,"q":"A passage states that a new bus route reduced average travel time by six minutes on weekdays, based on records from four weeks. Which conclusion is best supported?","c":["The route will reduce travel time for every passenger in every season.","The route reduced average weekday travel time during the four-week period studied.","The route eliminated all delays.","The route is the cheapest transport option in the city."],"a":1,"why":"The answer preserves the scope and limits of the evidence."},{"n":38,"q":"Which sentence best maintains a formal, precise tone?","c":["The machine was super cool and did a bunch of stuff.","The machine performed several functions and reduced processing time.","The machine was awesome at lots of things.","The machine kind of made the process better."],"a":1,"why":"It uses specific, formal wording without vague or informal expressions."},{"n":39,"q":"Choose the best revision for clarity. “The committee discussed the proposal with the engineers that had been revised.”","c":["The committee discussed the proposal with the engineers that had been revised.","The committee discussed the revised proposal with the engineers.","The committee discussed the proposal with the engineers, which had been revised.","The revised committee discussed the proposal with the engineers."],"a":1,"why":"The revision places revised next to proposal, the intended noun."},{"n":40,"q":"Which choice best describes the function of an example in an argument?","c":["It can illustrate a broader claim with a specific case.","It automatically proves a claim is universally true.","It replaces the need for any reasoning.","It introduces a completely unrelated topic."],"a":0,"why":"An example clarifies or supports a broader idea but does not prove a universal claim by itself."},{"n":41,"q":"Choose the best transition. “The old bridge was expensive to maintain. ___, the city repaired it rather than replacing it because the structure had historical value.”","c":["In addition","Nevertheless","For example","Similarly"],"a":1,"why":"The decision contrasts with the expected response to high maintenance costs."},{"n":42,"q":"Which option best revises the sentence for concision? “The reason the meeting was postponed was because the speaker was delayed.”","c":["The meeting was postponed because the speaker was delayed.","The reason for the postponement was due to the fact that the speaker was delayed.","The meeting was postponed for the reason that the speaker was delayed.","Because of the speaker being delayed was the reason the meeting was postponed."],"a":0,"why":"It removes the redundant phrase “the reason … was because.”"}]}]}]},"s21-practice-test-2":{"session":21,"title":"English Practice Test 2","intro":"A full-length, original 85-item practice test that follows the 2026 EST I HS Literacy structure: three 30-minute modules (Writing 35, Reading 25, Reading and Writing 25). The 12 drills at the end are untimed and do not count toward the 85-item score.","perModule":30,"modules":[{"name":"Module 1: Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage A: The Repair Café","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The Riverside Public Library began a repair café to help residents keep household items in use for longer. [1] The idea came from a survey, which found that many residents discarded small appliances because replacing them seemed easier than finding a repair service. [2] At monthly events, volunteers with practical experience help visitors diagnose problems with lamps, radios, bicycles, and clothing. [3] The volunteers do not promise to repair every item; instead, they explain what they can safely do and when a specialist is needed."},{"t":"p","x":"[4] During the first six months, the café welcomed 186 visitors and completed 119 repairs. [5] These figures suggest that the program is useful, but they do not prove that every repair prevented a purchase. Some visitors brought objects they had already decided to keep, while others came mainly to learn. [6] For that reason, the library added a short exit survey asking visitors what they planned to do before attending and what they did afterward."},{"t":"p","x":"[7] The survey showed that 42 visitors had expected to replace an item, and 29 of them later said they would keep it. [8] The result is encouraging, although the sample is small and depends on visitors’ own reports. [9] To improve the next evaluation, the library will contact a sample of participants three months later and ask whether the repaired objects still work. [10] Staff will also record the cost of tools, replacement parts, and volunteer training."},{"t":"p","x":"[11] The café has produced benefits that are harder to measure. Visitors often exchange tips, and some have returned to help new participants. [12] The library plans to publish a report that separates completed repairs, reported changes in purchasing plans, and longer-term outcomes. By distinguishing these measures, staff hope to avoid making claims that the available evidence cannot support. The project’s purpose is not to show that every broken object can be saved. It is to give residents a practical option, help them learn basic skills, and collect enough evidence to decide whether the service deserves to expand."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"q":"In sentence [1], which revision most clearly states the purpose of the repair café?","c":["The Riverside Public Library started a repair café to help residents extend the useful life of household items.","The Riverside Public Library began a repair café, which was a thing intended to help residents with items.","The Riverside Public Library started a repair café for the purpose of helping residents to keep items for longer periods of time.","The Riverside Public Library began a repair café, and the café was meant to help residents with household things."],"a":0,"why":"The revision states the purpose directly and removes wordy or vague phrasing."},{"n":2,"q":"Which punctuation correctly sets off the nonessential relative clause in sentence [2]?","c":["The idea came from a survey which found that many residents discarded small appliances.","The idea came from a survey; which found that many residents discarded small appliances.","The idea came from a survey, which found that many residents discarded small appliances.","The idea came from a survey: which found that many residents discarded small appliances."],"a":2,"why":"The nonessential relative clause adds information about the survey and takes a comma; a semicolon or colon cannot introduce it here."},{"n":3,"q":"Which choice best revises sentence [3] to make the contrast explicit?","c":["The volunteers do not promise to repair every item, because they explain what they can safely do and when a specialist is needed.","The volunteers do not promise to repair every item; instead, they explain what they can safely do and when a specialist is needed.","The volunteers do not promise to repair every item, instead they explain what they can safely do and when a specialist is needed.","The volunteers do not promise to repair every item instead they explain what they can safely do and when a specialist is needed."],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon joins the two independent clauses, and instead clearly signals the contrast."},{"n":4,"q":"Which transition best begins the sentence about the exit survey in sentence [6]?","c":["Consequently,","For example,","Likewise,","In contrast,"],"a":0,"why":"The survey was added in response to the limitation that the repair count alone could not show whether purchases were avoided."},{"n":5,"q":"Which verb form best maintains the past-perfect time relationship in sentence [7]?","c":["had expected","has expected","was expecting","expects"],"a":0,"why":"The plural subject is paired with the past perfect verb form had expected."},{"n":6,"q":"Which revision of sentence [8] best preserves a cautious, evidence-based tone?","c":["The result proves that the café prevents residents from buying replacement items.","The result is encouraging, although the sample is small and relies on visitors’ own reports.","The result is unquestionably successful because most visitors answered the survey.","The result is meaningless because the sample is small."],"a":1,"why":"This option acknowledges a positive result and its limitations without exaggerating either."},{"n":7,"q":"Which sentence would best follow sentence [10] to explain why the additional records matter?","c":["These records will help staff compare the program’s benefits with the resources it requires.","Repair cafés have become popular in many different countries.","Some residents prefer to buy new objects when their old ones break.","The library also plans to repaint the meeting room next year."],"a":0,"why":"The sentence logically connects cost tracking to evaluation of the program."},{"n":8,"q":"Which choice makes the three actions in sentence [10] parallel?","c":["the cost of tools, replacement parts, and training volunteers","the cost of purchasing tools, replacing parts, and training volunteers","tools, replacing parts, and volunteer training","to purchase tools, replacement parts, and training volunteers"],"a":1,"why":"The three gerund phrases—purchasing tools, replacing parts, and training volunteers—follow the same grammatical pattern."},{"n":9,"q":"Which punctuation is correct in this revision of sentence [11]?","c":["Visitors often exchange tips; and some have returned to help new participants.","Visitors often exchange tips, some have returned to help new participants.","Visitors often exchange tips, and some have returned to help new participants.","Visitors often exchange tips: and some have returned to help new participants."],"a":2,"why":"A comma plus and correctly joins two independent clauses."},{"n":10,"q":"Which pronoun best completes this sentence based on the passage? “The library added a survey so that ___ could compare visitors’ plans before and after an event.”","c":["they","it","them","their"],"a":1,"why":"The singular organization, the library, takes the singular pronoun it."},{"n":11,"q":"Which word best completes the sentence? “The café’s results are promising, but the library must ___ the findings over time.”","c":["verify","vary","justify","multiply"],"a":0,"why":"Verify means check whether the findings hold up; it matches the plan for follow-up evidence."},{"n":12,"q":"Which sentence best concludes the passage?","c":["The library should immediately open repair cafés in every neighborhood.","The project has already proved that repair services always reduce waste.","By measuring both immediate repairs and longer-term outcomes, the library can make a more informed decision about expanding the café.","Repair cafés are more enjoyable than traditional library activities."],"a":2,"why":"This conclusion synthesizes the passage’s emphasis on practical service and careful evaluation."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage B: Listening To The Town","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"In the town of Larkhaven, a group of students has begun recording the sounds of places that are changing. [13] Their project, called Listening to the Town, began when a planned road improvement threatened to alter a small market square. [14] Rather than argue only from photographs, the students decided to document the square’s daily soundscape: vendors calling to customers, delivery carts crossing the paving stones, and the bell of a nearby workshop."},{"t":"p","x":"[15] The students use handheld recorders and note the time, weather, and location of each recording. [16] A sound by itself can be difficult to interpret, so they also interview people who use the square. [17] One shopkeeper explained that the workshop bell has marked the end of the working day for decades; a younger resident said that she had never noticed it until the project drew her attention to it."},{"t":"p","x":"[18] The archive is not intended to prove that the square should never change. Instead, it gives planners a record of ordinary activities that a traffic diagram might overlook. [19] The students have also learned that recordings can create ethical problems. A microphone may capture a private conversation even when the recorder is aimed at a public space. [20] The group therefore asks permission before using identifiable voices and removes private conversations from its public collection."},{"t":"p","x":"[21] At the end of the term, the students will present a short audio walk that lets listeners compare several locations. [22] The project coordinator hopes the archive will help residents discuss which features of the square matter most and which changes could preserve its character while improving access. [23] By combining recordings, interviews, and careful consent procedures, the students are treating local memory as evidence that can inform planning rather than as a reason to reject every proposal. The archive is updated as the square changes, so future listeners can compare what remains with what has disappeared."}]},"questions":[{"n":13,"q":"In sentence [13], which revision correctly maintains the past-tense narration?","c":["begins when a planned road improvement threatens","began when a planned road improvement threatened","began when a planned road improvement threatens","has begun when a planned road improvement threatened"],"a":1,"why":"The passage narrates the project’s origin in the past, so began and threatened maintain tense consistency."},{"n":14,"q":"Which transition best signals that the audio archive offers an alternative to relying only on photographs? “The archive is not intended to prove that the square should never change. ___, it gives planners a record of ordinary activities.”","c":["Instead","For example","Similarly","As a result"],"a":0,"why":"Instead signals that the archive serves a different purpose: it records local activity without insisting that change must stop."},{"n":15,"q":"Which choice preserves correct punctuation in sentence [15]?","c":["The students use handheld recorders and note: the time, weather, and location of each recording.","The students use handheld recorders and note the time; weather; and location of each recording.","The students use handheld recorders and note the time, weather, and location of each recording.","The students use handheld recorders and note, the time, weather, and location of each recording."],"a":2,"why":"No colon is needed because note takes a direct object; commas separate the simple list."},{"n":16,"q":"Which revision best clarifies the relationship between sound and interpretation in sentence [16]?","c":["Because a sound alone can be difficult to interpret, the students also interview people who use the square.","A sound by itself can be difficult to interpret, the students also interview people who use the square.","A sound by itself can be difficult to interpret; because the students also interview people who use the square.","A sound by itself can be difficult to interpret and because they also interview people who use the square."],"a":0,"why":"The dependent clause explains why the interviews supplement the recordings."},{"n":17,"q":"Which word best describes the archive’s collection of recordings under the consent rules?","c":["permanent","exclusive","comprehensive","selective"],"a":3,"why":"The archive records chosen sounds and interviews under consent rules; selective fits the careful, limited collection."},{"n":18,"q":"Which choice best revises sentence [18] to avoid an overly broad claim?","c":["The archive proves that the square should never change.","The archive records ordinary activities that a traffic diagram might not show, without requiring planners to reject every proposed change.","The archive means that road improvements are always harmful.","The archive makes all residents agree about the square."],"a":1,"why":"The revision keeps the passage’s balanced point: the archive informs planning but does not rule out change."},{"n":19,"q":"Which choice best corrects the modifier in this sentence? “Aiming the recorder toward the square, a private conversation was sometimes captured.”","c":["Aiming the recorder toward the square, a private conversation was sometimes captured.","A private conversation, aiming the recorder toward the square, was sometimes captured.","When students aimed the recorder toward the square, it sometimes captured a private conversation.","A private conversation was sometimes captured, aiming the recorder toward the square."],"a":2,"why":"The students aim the recorder; naming them as the subject fixes the dangling modifier."},{"n":20,"q":"Which revision best combines the ideas in sentence [21] and sentence [22]?","c":["At the end of the term, the students will present an audio walk, and the coordinator hopes it will help residents discuss which features matter and which changes could preserve the square’s character.","At the end of the term, the students will present an audio walk, the coordinator hopes it will help residents discuss which features matter.","At the end of the term, the students will present an audio walk; and because the coordinator hopes it will help residents.","The students will present an audio walk at the end of the term the coordinator hopes it will help residents discuss features."],"a":0,"why":"The coordinated sentence preserves both ideas without creating a comma splice or fragment."},{"n":21,"q":"Which choice correctly shows possession in the phrase “the recordings belonging to several students”?","c":["the students recordings","the students' recordings","the student's recordings","the students's recordings"],"a":1,"why":"The plural noun students takes an apostrophe after the final s to show plural possession."},{"n":22,"q":"Which revision is most concise?","c":["The group therefore asks permission before using identifiable voices and removes private conversations from its public collection.","The group therefore asks for permission before using voices that are able to be identified by others and removes conversations that are private.","The group therefore asks permission in advance before using identifiable voices and removes private conversations from the public collection that it makes available.","The group, therefore, makes a request for permission before using voices that can be identified and removes conversations that are private."],"a":0,"why":"The original is already concise and precise; the other options add unnecessary wording."},{"n":23,"q":"What is the main rhetorical purpose of sentence [23]?","c":["To claim that local memory should override all technical planning","To summarize how several forms of evidence and ethical safeguards support informed planning","To argue that public recordings are more reliable than interviews","To suggest that the project should stop before the end of the term"],"a":1,"why":"The sentence summarizes the project’s combined methods and explains their role in planning."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage C: Clearer Museum Labels","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A regional museum recently redesigned its exhibition labels after visitors reported that they found some displays difficult to follow. [24] The old labels often placed dates, technical terms, and background information in a single dense paragraph. [25] The museum’s new approach begins with a short statement of the object’s significance, followed by two or three details that support it. [26] Visitors who want more information can scan a code for a longer explanation."},{"t":"p","x":"[27] The museum tested the labels in two galleries over four weekends. Staff observed whether visitors paused to read, asked questions, or moved on quickly. [28] They also invited volunteers to answer a short comprehension check after viewing a display. [29] The first results were promising: more visitors could explain why an object mattered, and fewer reported feeling overwhelmed by the amount of text. [30] Still, the team did not treat the trial as a final verdict. The galleries differed in lighting and crowding, and the visitors who completed the check chose to participate."},{"t":"p","x":"[31] For the next phase, the museum will test the same label format in a gallery with more complex scientific objects. [32] Curators will compare visitor responses while keeping the basic label structure consistent. [33] They will also ask whether the optional longer explanation attracts the people who need it or mainly those who already enjoy reading museum text. [34] This question matters because a design can appear successful among confident visitors while leaving other visitors behind."},{"t":"p","x":"[35] The museum’s goal is not to reduce every explanation to a slogan. It is to help visitors grasp the central idea quickly and then choose how deeply they want to explore. If the next trial confirms the early results, the museum will revise the labels gradually and continue gathering feedback. The staff see clear writing as part of access: a well-designed label should invite curiosity without assuming that every visitor arrives with the same knowledge."}]},"questions":[{"n":24,"q":"Which choice best corrects sentence [24] for clarity?","c":["The old labels often placed dates, technical terms, and background information in one dense paragraph.","The old labels often placed dates, technical terms, and background information, all of which were together in one dense paragraph.","The old labels often placed dates, technical terms, and background information in a paragraph that was dense in nature.","The old labels often placed dates, technical terms, and background information, placing them all in a dense paragraph."],"a":0,"why":"The revision removes redundancy and expresses the idea directly."},{"n":25,"q":"Which transition best begins sentence [25] to show a change from the old method to the new method?","c":["By contrast,","For example,","Similarly,","In addition,"],"a":0,"why":"The new approach differs from the dense old labels, so a contrast transition fits."},{"n":26,"q":"Which choice correctly revises sentence [25]?","c":["The museum’s new approach begins with a short statement of the object’s significance; followed by two or three details.","The museum’s new approach begins with a short statement of the object’s significance: followed by two or three details.","The museum’s new approach begins with a short statement of the object’s significance, followed by two or three details that support it.","The museum’s new approach begins with: a short statement of the object’s significance, followed by two or three details."],"a":2,"why":"The sentence uses a participial phrase after the main clause; a colon is not appropriate before followed."},{"n":27,"q":"Which verb form best maintains the past-time frame in a revision of sentence [27]?","c":["observes","have observed","observed","observing"],"a":2,"why":"The passage describes a completed trial in the past, so observed maintains tense consistency."},{"n":28,"q":"Which sentence best explains why the museum uses a comprehension check?","c":["It measures whether visitors can explain the significance of an object after reading the label.","It proves that every visitor likes the new label format.","It eliminates the need to observe visitors in the galleries.","It ensures that visitors will read every word on every display."],"a":0,"why":"The check directly assesses whether visitors understand the label’s main point."},{"n":29,"q":"Which transition best begins sentence [30]?","c":["Even so,","For instance,","As a result,","Likewise,"],"a":0,"why":"The sentence limits otherwise promising findings by noting weaknesses in the trial."},{"n":30,"q":"Which revision best combines the ideas in sentence [31] and sentence [32]?","c":["For the next phase, the museum will test the same label format in a gallery with more complex scientific objects and compare visitor responses while keeping the basic structure consistent.","For the next phase, the museum will test the same label format, it will compare visitor responses while keeping the basic structure consistent.","For the next phase, the museum will test the same label format; because the gallery has more complex scientific objects and compare responses.","For the next phase, the museum will test the same label format in a gallery with more complex scientific objects, comparing visitor responses, and the basic structure will be consistent."],"a":0,"why":"The sentence coordinates the next test and the controlled comparison clearly."},{"n":31,"q":"Which choice correctly uses the relative clause in sentence [33]?","c":["They will ask whether the optional explanation attracts visitors who need it or mainly those who already enjoy reading museum text.","They will ask whether the optional explanation attracts visitors, who need it, or mainly those who already enjoy reading museum text.","They will ask whether the optional explanation attracts visitors who need it, or, mainly those who already enjoy reading museum text.","They will ask whether the optional explanation attracts visitors, who need it or mainly those who already enjoy reading museum text."],"a":0,"why":"The clause visitors who need it is essential to the meaning and should not be separated by commas."},{"n":32,"q":"Which choice best completes this sentence? “A design can appear successful among confident visitors while ___ other visitors behind.”","c":["leave","leaving","leaves","left"],"a":1,"why":"The while-clause uses the participle leaving to express a simultaneous result."},{"n":33,"q":"Which sentence should the writer add after sentence [34] to develop the point about access?","c":["For this reason, the museum will compare responses from visitors with different levels of prior knowledge.","The museum gift shop sells postcards of several popular paintings.","Some visitors arrive at the museum by bus, while others walk.","The museum was founded more than a century ago."],"a":0,"why":"The sentence directly develops the concern that different visitors may need different levels of explanation."},{"n":34,"q":"Which choice best revises the final two sentences for a logical conclusion?","c":["The museum will revise the labels gradually if the next trial supports the early results, while continuing to gather feedback about whether the labels invite visitors with different levels of knowledge.","The museum will revise every label immediately because the first trial proved the new design works for all visitors.","The museum will stop gathering feedback once the labels have been changed.","The museum should avoid using longer explanations because some visitors do not read them."],"a":0,"why":"This conclusion respects the preliminary nature of the results and preserves the museum’s goal of accessible interpretation."},{"n":35,"q":"Which punctuation best sets off the phrase “a well-designed label should invite curiosity” as an explanatory conclusion after a complete clause?","c":["The staff see clear writing as part of access—a well-designed label should invite curiosity without assuming that every visitor arrives with the same knowledge.","The staff see clear writing as part of access, a well-designed label should invite curiosity without assuming that every visitor arrives with the same knowledge.","The staff see clear writing as part of access; because a well-designed label should invite curiosity without assuming that every visitor arrives with the same knowledge.","The staff see clear writing as part of access? a well-designed label should invite curiosity without assuming that every visitor arrives with the same knowledge."],"a":0,"why":"A dash can introduce an explanation or restatement; the comma would create a splice."}]}]},{"name":"Module 2: Reading","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage A: Temporary Green Spaces","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"For many years, planners treated vacant lots as temporary gaps in the urban landscape. A lot might wait for a future building, serve briefly as a parking area, or remain fenced while its ownership was disputed. In several neighborhoods, residents began using such spaces for small gardens, play areas, and outdoor gatherings. These informal uses were sometimes described as disorderly because they did not follow a single citywide design. Yet the spaces often met needs that formal parks, located farther away, could not meet as easily."},{"t":"p","x":"A study of twelve temporary green spaces in the city of Marston followed their use over two summers. Researchers visited each site at set times, counting visitors and recording activities. They found that the busiest spaces were not necessarily the largest. Lots near apartment buildings attracted short visits throughout the day, while a larger site beside a transit stop drew fewer visitors but hosted occasional community events. The researchers concluded that location and the type of activity mattered alongside size."},{"t":"p","x":"The study also documented problems. Some gardens lacked reliable access to water, and volunteers sometimes struggled to maintain paths or remove litter. Neighbors disagreed about whether music and evening gatherings should be allowed. In two locations, a lack of clear responsibility meant that repairs took weeks. The researchers argued that these difficulties did not make temporary green spaces useless; they showed that even small projects need rules, maintenance, and a clear way to resolve complaints."},{"t":"p","x":"The report cautioned against treating the twelve sites as a representative sample of every neighborhood. Residents who volunteered for interviews may have been more supportive than those who avoided the spaces, and the two summers brought unusually mild weather. The team recommended longer studies that include different seasons and compare temporary lots with established parks. It also suggested that cities publish simple agreements defining who waters plants, maintains equipment, and responds to concerns."},{"t":"p","x":"The strongest conclusion was practical rather than celebratory. Temporary green spaces can provide nearby places to meet, rest, and grow plants, but their value depends on how people use them and how well they are managed. A vacant lot does not become a successful public space simply because someone adds benches or flowers. It becomes useful when a design responds to local needs and when residents and officials share responsibility for keeping it accessible. These recommendations are intended to help cities learn from small experiments before committing to permanent construction."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"q":"Which choice best states the central idea of the Marston passage?","c":["Temporary green spaces can meet local needs, but their success depends on context, maintenance, and shared responsibility.","Every vacant lot should become a permanent park regardless of neighborhood needs.","Large parks always attract more visitors than small green spaces.","Residents should manage public spaces without support from city officials."],"a":0,"why":"The passage balances potential benefits with practical conditions for success."},{"n":37,"q":"Why does the author mention the twelve sites that researchers followed over two summers?","c":["To prove that all cities have the same patterns of park use","To provide evidence about how different temporary spaces were used","To show that the largest lot had the greatest number of visitors","To explain why the study ended before winter"],"a":1,"why":"The observations provide the basis for comparing how location and activity relate to use."},{"n":38,"q":"Which inference is best supported by the finding that smaller lots near apartments attracted short visits throughout the day?","c":["Residents may value easy access even when a green space is small.","People never visit large green spaces for recreation.","Apartment residents do not use established parks.","Small lots require no maintenance."],"a":0,"why":"Frequent short visits suggest proximity can matter alongside size."},{"n":39,"q":"As used in paragraph 2, “hosted” most nearly means:","c":["provided a place for","paid for","prevented","advertised"],"a":0,"why":"The larger site served as the location for community events."},{"n":40,"q":"What role does the discussion of water access, litter, and disagreements play in the passage?","c":["It disproves the claim that residents used the spaces.","It shows that management challenges must be addressed for the spaces to remain useful.","It proves that formal parks never experience problems.","It shifts the passage to an unrelated topic."],"a":1,"why":"These examples qualify the benefits and support the recommendation for clear rules and maintenance."},{"n":41,"q":"Which statement best describes the researchers’ approach to evidence?","c":["They treated interview responses as a complete representation of every resident.","They acknowledged sampling and weather limitations and recommended further comparison.","They relied entirely on photographs taken by volunteers.","They ignored differences between the sites."],"a":1,"why":"The authors explicitly identify limitations and recommend longer studies across seasons."},{"n":42,"q":"Which additional evidence would most directly help determine whether temporary green spaces serve needs that established parks do not meet?","c":["A comparison of travel distance, visit patterns, and activities at temporary sites and established parks","A list of the flower colors selected by each volunteer","A survey asking only the most frequent visitors whether they like gardening","A count of vacant lots in a different country with no comparison of use"],"a":0,"why":"This comparison directly tests whether proximity and activity patterns differ from established parks."},{"n":43,"q":"What is the author’s primary purpose?","c":["To persuade readers that all temporary green spaces should be closed","To present a measured account of benefits and conditions that influence the success of temporary green spaces","To describe the history of one particular flower species","To argue that city planning should exclude residents"],"a":1,"why":"The passage explains benefits, challenges, study limitations, and practical recommendations."},{"n":44,"q":"Which choice best describes the relationship between paragraph 3 and paragraph 4?","c":["Paragraph 3 lists management challenges; paragraph 4 explains why the study’s conclusions must remain limited.","Paragraph 3 presents a conclusion; paragraph 4 contradicts every detail in it.","Paragraph 3 describes the methods; paragraph 4 introduces an unrelated experiment.","Paragraph 3 argues for permanent parks; paragraph 4 argues against all public spaces."],"a":0,"why":"The paragraphs move from practical difficulties to limits in generalizing the findings."},{"n":45,"q":"Which conclusion is best supported by the passage?","c":["Adding benches is sufficient to make any vacant lot successful.","Temporary green spaces can work when their design responds to local needs and maintenance responsibilities are clear.","Community spaces should never host evening activities.","The study proves that temporary spaces outperform established parks."],"a":1,"why":"This is the passage’s cautious, practical conclusion."},{"n":46,"q":"Which statement would the researchers most likely agree with?","c":["Small studies are useless unless they include every resident.","Evidence about use should be interpreted alongside the conditions under which it was collected.","Any disagreement among neighbors means a project has failed.","Weather has no influence on how people use public spaces."],"a":1,"why":"The researchers repeatedly consider context, sampling, weather, and site differences."},{"n":47,"q":"Which detail most directly supports the claim that clear responsibility matters?","c":["Some lots waited weeks for repairs because no one clearly owned the task.","Some spaces were beside apartment buildings.","The study followed the lots for two summers.","One site hosted occasional community events."],"a":0,"why":"The delay in repairs directly links unclear responsibility to maintenance problems."},{"n":48,"q":"Which revision best summarizes the report without overstating its findings?","c":["The study proves temporary green spaces are the best use for every vacant lot.","The study suggests temporary green spaces can be valuable, while showing that access, maintenance, and local context shape their success.","The study shows that residents dislike formal parks.","The study demonstrates that all twelve sites were equally successful."],"a":1,"why":"The summary preserves both the positive findings and the limitations."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage B: Citizen Science And Reliable Data","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Citizen science projects invite members of the public to help collect observations that researchers can use. Volunteers may count birds, photograph flowering plants, measure rainfall, or record the timing of seasonal events. These contributions can extend a study across more places and days than a small research team could cover alone. The method is especially useful when the question depends on repeated observations over a wide area."},{"t":"p","x":"The approach also creates a challenge: more observations do not automatically produce better evidence. Volunteers may use different methods, identify species inconsistently, or submit records from places that are easy to reach rather than places that need study. A large database can therefore look impressive while containing gaps or uneven levels of reliability. Good projects address these risks before analysis begins."},{"t":"p","x":"One coastal project trained volunteers to record the first date each spring that a particular plant flowered. The organizers provided photographs showing the plant at different stages, asked volunteers to record location and date, and marked uncertain identifications for expert review. They also compared volunteer records with observations from a smaller group of trained botanists. The two groups did not agree on every record, but the comparison helped researchers identify which instructions needed clarification."},{"t":"p","x":"The project’s organizers published a guide explaining how they handled missing observations and uncertain records. They did not replace missing dates with averages, because doing so would make an estimate look like a direct observation. Instead, they analyzed the confirmed records and stated where the data were too sparse to support a conclusion. This approach reduced the apparent completeness of the dataset but made its limits easier to understand."},{"t":"p","x":"Citizen science can also influence participants. Some volunteers reported learning to distinguish species they had previously grouped together, while others became interested in local conservation decisions. Those outcomes matter, but they are different from the scientific question of whether the data support a particular claim. A project may teach valuable skills even when its records cannot answer every research question."},{"t":"p","x":"The authors recommend judging a citizen science project by the quality of its procedures, not merely by the number of people who participate. Clear instructions, expert checks, transparent treatment of uncertainty, and a plan for analyzing the results all help turn public observations into useful evidence. Participation expands the reach of research; careful methods determine what the resulting evidence can show. The same principles apply whether volunteers observe wildlife, weather, or changes in the built environment."},{"t":"h","x":"Audit of 100 submitted records:"},{"t":"table","x":{"head":["Outcome in the audit sample","Number of records"],"rows":[["Confirmed without change","84"],["Corrected after expert review","11"],["Could not be verified","5"]]}}]},"questions":[{"n":49,"q":"Which choice best states the central idea of the citizen science passage?","c":["Citizen science produces reliable evidence automatically when many people participate.","Public participation can expand research, but careful procedures are needed to make the resulting observations useful.","Only professional scientists should collect observations about plants.","Researchers should replace missing observations with averages to complete a database."],"a":1,"why":"The passage emphasizes both the reach of volunteer data and the need for quality controls."},{"n":50,"q":"Why did the coastal project provide photographs of the plant at different stages?","c":["To make the records look more attractive","To help volunteers identify the plant more consistently","To eliminate the need to record dates","To encourage volunteers to submit uncertain observations without review"],"a":1,"why":"The images supported consistent identification and clarified instructions."},{"n":51,"q":"What does the passage imply about a large database with uneven reliability?","c":["Its size alone does not guarantee that it can support a sound conclusion.","It must be more accurate than a smaller dataset.","It cannot contain any useful information.","It should be published without explaining how the records were collected."],"a":0,"why":"The author warns that volume cannot compensate for gaps or inconsistent methods."},{"n":52,"q":"As used in paragraph 3, “clarification” most nearly means:","c":["an explanation made clearer","a deliberate omission","a scientific disagreement","a final prediction"],"a":0,"why":"The instructions needed to become clearer after researchers reviewed disagreements between observers."},{"n":53,"q":"Why did organizers avoid replacing missing flowering dates with averages?","c":["Average dates are always inaccurate.","They did not want estimates to appear to be direct observations.","They wanted the database to contain no numerical information.","They believed volunteers should not record dates."],"a":1,"why":"The text explicitly says replacing missing dates would make estimates look like observations."},{"n":54,"q":"Which statement best distinguishes participant learning from the scientific value of the data?","c":["Learning outcomes may be valuable even when the records cannot answer every research question.","Participant learning automatically proves the scientific claim.","Scientific data are useful only when every volunteer learns a new skill.","Learning and scientific evidence are identical measures."],"a":0,"why":"The passage separates educational benefits from whether the records support a particular claim."},{"n":55,"q":"Which additional procedure would best strengthen the reliability of a similar project?","c":["Let each volunteer invent a separate method.","Provide common instructions and review a sample of observations with trained experts.","Delete uncertain records without documenting the decision.","Publish only records that support the expected result."],"a":1,"why":"Consistent procedures and expert review address the reliability issues described."},{"n":56,"q":"Which statement best describes the tone of the passage?","c":["Dismissive of public participation","Enthusiastic but uncritical","Measured and explanatory","Angry about scientific uncertainty"],"a":2,"why":"The author recognizes benefits while carefully discussing limitations and safeguards."},{"n":57,"q":"A later audit examined 100 submitted records. According to the table, which statement is supported?","c":["Eighty-four records were confirmed without change.","All sixteen records not confirmed without change were proven false.","Eleven records were impossible to verify.","Five records were corrected by averaging missing dates."],"a":0,"why":"The table shows 84 records confirmed without change; it does not say every other record was false or describe those other categories as the distractors claim."},{"n":58,"q":"Which claim is best supported by the passage?","c":["The number of participants is the only meaningful measure of project quality.","Clear methods and transparent handling of uncertainty help make public observations usable.","Volunteer observations should never be compared with expert observations.","Every citizen science project can answer every research question."],"a":1,"why":"This statement captures the author’s recommendations."},{"n":59,"q":"How do the two passages differ in their main emphasis?","c":["The Marston passage focuses on managing public spaces, while the citizen science passage focuses on the quality of collected observations.","The Marston passage rejects all community involvement, while the citizen science passage supports it.","Both passages argue that numerical evidence is always misleading.","The Marston passage describes a laboratory experiment, while the citizen science passage describes a fictional story."],"a":0,"why":"One examines local-space use and management; the other examines volunteer data collection and reliability."},{"n":60,"q":"Which principle is supported by both passages?","c":["Community projects succeed whenever they attract many participants.","Local initiatives benefit from clear procedures, careful evaluation, and honest acknowledgment of limitations.","Researchers should avoid gathering data from residents.","Small projects should be expanded before their results are reviewed."],"a":1,"why":"Both texts argue that community participation needs sound procedures and cautious interpretation."}]}]},{"name":"Module 3: Reading And Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":null,"questions":[{"n":61,"q":"Stimulus: The excerpt comes from a laboratory progress report prepared for a review committee. The laboratory’s first trial produced inconsistent readings. The team recalibrated the sensors before repeating the experiment. Which transition best links the two sentences?","c":["As a result","Likewise","For example","In contrast"],"a":0,"why":"The recalibration responds to the inconsistent readings."},{"n":62,"q":"Stimulus: The sentence appears in a school archive’s annual summary of its collection. The archive contains letters, maps, and photographs from the expedition. Each item ___ catalogued before researchers can request it. Which verb agrees with the subject?","c":["are","were","is","have been"],"a":2,"why":"The subject each item is singular, so is agrees."},{"n":63,"q":"Stimulus: The passage is part of a profile describing a recent design award. The architect, ___ design won the regional award, spoke to students about accessible buildings. Which word correctly shows possession?","c":["who","whom","whose","which"],"a":2,"why":"Whose shows that the design belongs to the architect."},{"n":64,"q":"Stimulus: The following line appears in a field report written after a site visit. The team collected samples from three locations, ___ the results varied by soil type. Which punctuation best joins the independent clauses?","c":[",",";","—,","because;"],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon joins two independent clauses without a conjunction."},{"n":65,"q":"Stimulus: The example comes from a guide for students completing a research project. The report was not only detailed but also ___ for readers without technical training. Which word best completes the parallel construction?","c":["accessible","accessing","to access","accessibility"],"a":0,"why":"The adjectives detailed and accessible form a parallel pair."},{"n":66,"q":"Stimulus: The sentence appears in a report about an analyst’s work with visual evidence. After reviewing the chart, ___ noticed that the eastern site had the highest reading. Which option corrects the dangling modifier?","c":["the conclusion was obvious","the analyst noticed","the highest reading was noticed","the chart was reviewed by the analyst who noticed"],"a":1,"why":"The analyst is the person reviewing the chart, so the analyst should be the subject."},{"n":67,"q":"Stimulus: The excerpt summarizes a short evaluation conducted by a museum team. The museum extended its hours for one month. Visitor numbers rose during that period, although a holiday festival also took place. Which conclusion is best supported?","c":["The extended hours alone caused the increase.","The festival had no effect on attendance.","Attendance rose, but the evidence does not isolate the effect of extended hours.","The museum will always have more visitors at night."],"a":2,"why":"The festival is a confounding factor, so the cause cannot be isolated."},{"n":68,"q":"Stimulus: The passage comes from minutes recorded after a committee meeting last week. The committee reviewed the proposal carefully. ___, it requested additional cost estimates before voting. Which transition best fits?","c":["Nevertheless","For instance","Therefore","Similarly"],"a":2,"why":"The committee’s request follows from its review and need for more information."},{"n":69,"q":"Stimulus: The sentence appears in a biographical note prepared for a local history archive. The historian found a diary that described the journey. The diary’s author, ___ name appears on the final page, was later identified. Which word fits?","c":["who","whom","whose","which"],"a":2,"why":"Whose marks possession of the name."},{"n":70,"q":"Stimulus: The excerpt comes from a comparison of two stages in a research project. The seedlings grew quickly; the mature plants, ___, needed less frequent watering. Which transition signals a contrast?","c":["for example","by contrast","as a result","in addition"],"a":1,"why":"The sentence contrasts seedlings with mature plants."},{"n":71,"q":"Stimulus: The passage appears in an editor’s note explaining a revision. The editor removed two repeated phrases and shortened the introduction. The revised article was easier to follow. What is the main function of the second sentence?","c":["It gives a result of the revisions.","It contradicts the first sentence.","It introduces an unrelated topic.","It explains why the editor rejected the article."],"a":0,"why":"The improved readability is presented as the outcome of the edits."},{"n":72,"q":"Stimulus: The sentence is part of a field team’s standard data-collection procedure. The field team recorded the date, location, and weather conditions for every sample. during each visit. Which revision best improves the sentence?","c":["The field team recorded the date, location, and weather conditions for every sample.","The field team recorded the date, the location, and weather conditions, for every sample.","The field team recorded: the date, location, and weather conditions for every sample.","The field team recorded the date; location; and weather conditions for every sample."],"a":0,"why":"The original sentence correctly uses commas in a simple series."},{"n":73,"q":"Stimulus: The following statements summarize findings from two school garden reports. Text A argues that a school garden offers practical science lessons. Text B notes that the garden also gives students a shared responsibility. Which statement best synthesizes the texts?","c":["The garden teaches science and can build a sense of shared responsibility.","The garden is useful only as a science laboratory.","Students should not share garden tasks.","Both texts argue that gardening is too expensive."],"a":0,"why":"The synthesis combines the two compatible central ideas."},{"n":74,"q":"Stimulus: The word appears in a technical note about an unfinished model. The engineer called the first model “provisional” because several measurements were still missing. at the time. As used here, “provisional” most nearly means:","c":["temporary","careless","permanent","irrelevant"],"a":0,"why":"The model may change when missing measurements become available."},{"n":75,"q":"Stimulus: The excerpt comes from a customer-service report about revised instructions. The company revised its instructions after customers reported confusion. The new version uses shorter steps and includes diagrams. Which statement best describes the relationship between the sentences?","c":["The second explains how the company responded to the problem.","The second disproves that customers were confused.","The first gives a result of the second.","The sentences describe unrelated changes."],"a":0,"why":"The second sentence specifies the revisions mentioned in the first."},{"n":76,"q":"Stimulus: The passage is part of a report describing a collection’s recent donation. The collection includes several rare books. One of them ___ donated by a former student. Which verb form is correct?","c":["were","are","was","have been"],"a":2,"why":"One is singular, so was agrees."},{"n":77,"q":"Stimulus: The sentence appears in a research summary written after a pilot study. The results were promising, ___ the researchers warned that the sample included only eight participants. Which transition best fits?","c":["because","but","for example","therefore"],"a":1,"why":"The second clause qualifies the positive result."},{"n":78,"q":"Stimulus: The guide is intended for volunteers who are learning to identify local birds. The guide explains how to identify the bird, where it nests, and ___ it feeds. Which choice completes the parallel list?","c":["what food it eats","its feeding","to eat food","where feeding occurs it"],"a":0,"why":"The list uses parallel question clauses: how, where, and what."},{"n":79,"q":"Stimulus: The table is part of a city transport report comparing monthly counts. A city report shows that bicycle trips increased from 1,200 in April to 1,500 in May. The report covers only those two months. Which statement is supported?","c":["Bicycle trips increased by 300 between April and May.","Trips will increase every month.","The city has no traffic congestion.","The increase was caused by a new law."],"a":0,"why":"The numbers support only the difference between the two months."},{"n":80,"q":"Stimulus: The excerpt comes from a literary review comparing an author’s early and later work. The novelist’s early stories were brief and humorous. Her later work explored memory, migration, and family history. What can reasonably be inferred?","c":["Her writing interests changed or broadened over time.","She stopped writing fiction entirely.","Readers disliked her early stories.","Her later work was always autobiographical."],"a":0,"why":"The described themes suggest a broader range in later work, but not the stronger claims."},{"n":81,"q":"Stimulus: The sentence appears in a laboratory report describing a follow-up check. The lab’s results were consistent across three trials. The team then repeated the test with a different instrument to check whether the pattern held. What is the purpose of the second sentence?","c":["To challenge the reliability of all previous measurements","To test whether the result persists under a different measurement condition","To show that the first trials were not completed","To replace the need for evidence"],"a":1,"why":"The team checks whether the pattern survives a change in instrument."},{"n":82,"q":"Stimulus: The sentence is from an announcement thanking people who prepared an exhibition. The director thanked the volunteers, ___ had worked through the weekend to prepare the exhibition. Which relative pronoun is correct?","c":["which","who","whose","where"],"a":1,"why":"Who refers to people and acts as the subject of had worked."},{"n":83,"q":"Stimulus: The excerpt appears in a local news report about a bridge repair. The bridge reopened in June. Repairs had taken longer than expected because workers discovered damage beneath the road surface. Which statement best explains the delay?","c":["Workers discovered additional damage during repairs.","The bridge was never closed.","The road surface had no damage.","The bridge reopened before repairs began."],"a":0,"why":"The passage directly identifies the discovery as the reason for the delay."},{"n":84,"q":"Stimulus: The sentence is part of a survey-design guide for a community project. The survey asked residents whether they used the park, how often they visited, and what improvements they wanted. Which feature makes the list effective?","c":["It combines unrelated verb tenses.","It presents three parallel questions about park use and preferences.","It uses a semicolon between every item.","It repeats the same question three times."],"a":1,"why":"The parallel question clauses make the survey topics easy to follow."},{"n":85,"q":"Stimulus: The statement appears in a researcher’s recommendation after a limited pilot. A researcher writes, “The pilot program may be worth expanding, but only after a longer trial includes winter observations.” Which statement best captures the researcher’s position?","c":["The program has failed and should end.","The program should expand immediately.","Expansion may be justified after more evidence is collected.","Winter observations are irrelevant."],"a":2,"why":"The recommendation is conditional on gathering more evidence."}]}]},{"name":"Drills","scored":false,"blocks":[{"passage":null,"questions":[{"n":86,"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","c":["After the trial ended, the researchers reviewed the notes.","After the trial ended the researchers, reviewed the notes.","After the trial, ended the researchers reviewed the notes.","After the trial ended; the researchers, reviewed the notes."],"a":0,"why":"A comma separates the introductory dependent clause from the independent clause."},{"n":87,"q":"Choose the correct word pair. “The new policy may ___ attendance; its long-term ___ remains uncertain.”","c":["effect / affect","affect / effect","affect / affect","effect / effect"],"a":1,"why":"Affect is a verb meaning influence; effect is a noun meaning result."},{"n":88,"q":"Which sentence uses a colon correctly?","c":["The team had one priority: to protect the samples.","The team had: one priority to protect the samples.","The team: had one priority, to protect the samples.","The team had one: priority to protect the samples."],"a":0,"why":"A colon follows a complete clause and introduces an explanation."},{"n":89,"q":"Which choice correctly shows the plural possessive?","c":["the two teacher's desks","the two teachers desks","the two teachers' desks","the two teachers's desks"],"a":2,"why":"The plural teachers takes an apostrophe after s."},{"n":90,"q":"Which option best avoids a comma splice?","c":["The trial ended, the team wrote a report.","The trial ended; the team wrote a report.","The trial ended, because; the team wrote a report.","The trial ended the team, wrote a report."],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon joins two independent clauses."},{"n":91,"q":"Which sentence uses a nonessential clause correctly?","c":["The bridge, which opened in 1932, still carries traffic.","The bridge which opened in 1932, still carries traffic.","The bridge, which opened in 1932 still carries traffic.","The bridge which, opened in 1932, still carries traffic."],"a":0,"why":"The nonessential clause needs commas on both sides."},{"n":92,"q":"Choose the most concise revision. “At this point in time, the team is currently reviewing the results.”","c":["At this point in time, the team is currently reviewing the results.","The team is reviewing the results now.","At the present time, the team is in the process of reviewing the results currently.","The team currently is at this point reviewing the results."],"a":1,"why":"Now removes redundant time expressions."},{"n":93,"q":"Which transition best completes the sentence? “The first sample was contaminated. ___, the team discarded it and collected another.”","c":["Likewise","As a result","In contrast","For example"],"a":1,"why":"Discarding the sample is a response to contamination."},{"n":94,"q":"Which choice is parallel? “The course asks students to read critically, write clearly, and ___.”","c":["careful listening","to listen carefully","listen carefully","listening carefully"],"a":2,"why":"The three verbs follow the same base-verb structure."},{"n":95,"q":"Which conclusion is justified by evidence from a single week of observations?","c":["The pattern will continue for decades.","The observations establish a permanent trend.","The pattern appeared during the week observed, but longer monitoring is needed to assess persistence.","No conclusion can ever be drawn from a week of observations."],"a":2,"why":"The answer respects the limited time span without dismissing the evidence entirely."},{"n":96,"q":"Which sentence best corrects the misplaced modifier? “The guide described the rare bird to the visitors with a bright orange beak.”","c":["The guide with a bright orange beak described the rare bird to the visitors.","The guide described the rare bird with a bright orange beak to the visitors.","The guide described with a bright orange beak the rare bird to the visitors.","The guide described the rare bird to the visitors, with a bright orange beak."],"a":1,"why":"The phrase with a bright orange beak belongs next to rare bird."},{"n":97,"q":"Which statement best describes strong evidence in an argument?","c":["It is vivid even if it is unrelated to the claim.","It supports the specific claim and comes from a relevant, credible source or observation.","It uses absolute language so readers feel certain.","It includes as many numbers as possible, regardless of context."],"a":1,"why":"Evidence must be relevant to the claim and reliable; quantity or dramatic language alone is not enough."}]}]}]},"s22-practice-test-3":{"session":22,"title":"Practice Test 3 (checkpoint)","intro":"A 30-question checkpoint: 10 Writing, 10 Reading and 10 mixed Reading and Writing items. Allow about 30 minutes. It is shorter than the official format.","perModule":null,"modules":[{"name":"Module A: Writing And Editing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage A: The Neighborhood Repair Cafe","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A neighborhood repair cafe began as a monthly table in a community hall. Residents brought lamps, torn bags, loose chair legs, and small appliances that no longer worked as expected. Volunteers did not promise to fix every item. Instead, they helped visitors identify the problem, decide whether a repair was safe, and learn which tools or parts might be needed. Some visitors completed the work themselves; others left with advice about finding a qualified technician."},{"t":"p","x":"The organizers expected the cafe to reduce waste, but they soon noticed another benefit. People who had arrived believing that a broken object was automatically useless began asking whether a damaged part could be replaced. A retired electrician showed a teenager how to test a plug safely, while a seamstress taught another visitor to reinforce a torn pocket. The exchanges gave people practical skills and created conversations among neighbors who had rarely met."},{"t":"p","x":"The cafe keeps a simple record of each visit. Volunteers note the item, the problem, whether the item was repaired, and whether the visitor learned a new skill. During its first six months, the cafe received 146 items. Volunteers repaired 82 on site, helped visitors make 31 additional repairs at home, and referred 18 cases to professionals. The remaining items could not be repaired safely or economically."},{"t":"p","x":"These numbers do not show exactly how much waste the cafe prevented. Some repaired items might otherwise have been stored rather than discarded, and the record does not track how long repairs lasted. The organizers plan to contact a sample of visitors three months after each visit. They also want to record the cost of replacement parts so that future reports distinguish inexpensive fixes from repairs that require substantial resources."},{"t":"p","x":"The cafe's purpose is not to make every object last forever. It is to help residents make informed decisions, gain confidence, and avoid replacing items when a safe, practical repair is possible."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Which revision is the most concise replacement for ‘began as a monthly table in a community hall’?","c":["started as a monthly repair event in a community hall","began at a time when each month a table was located in a hall","was first initially started as a monthly table in a hall","had its beginning start with a table once every month in a community hall"],"a":0,"why":"A states the idea directly and removes redundant wording."},{"n":2,"q":"Which transition best connects the repair cafe’s records to the limits of those records? ‘The cafe repaired many items. ___, the counts do not show exactly how much waste it prevented.’","c":["For example","Nevertheless","Similarly","In addition"],"a":1,"why":"Nevertheless signals that a positive result does not resolve the measurement limitation."},{"n":3,"q":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","c":["The record of visits show how often residents use the cafe.","The record of visits shows how often residents use the cafe.","The records of visits shows how often residents use the cafe.","The record of visits have shown how often residents use the cafe."],"a":1,"why":"The singular subject ‘record’ takes the singular verb ‘shows.’"},{"n":4,"q":"Which option correctly punctuates the sentence listing the cafe’s four recorded details?","c":["Volunteers note: the item, the problem, the repair result, and any new skill.","Volunteers note; the item, the problem, the repair result, and any new skill.","Volunteers note the item, the problem, the repair result, and any new skill.","Volunteers note, the item, the problem, the repair result and any new skill."],"a":2,"why":"A straightforward series takes commas; a colon after the verb would incorrectly split the clause."},{"n":5,"q":"Which sentence best expresses the purpose of paragraph 2?","c":["To explain how skill-sharing became an additional benefit of the cafe","To prove that every broken object can be repaired","To argue that only trained electricians should attend","To show that the cafe has stopped recording visits"],"a":0,"why":"Paragraph 2 describes visitors learning skills and connecting with neighbors."},{"n":6,"q":"Which revision fixes the parallelism error? ‘Visitors could learn to test a plug, reinforcing a seam, and how to choose a safe repair.’","c":["Visitors could learn testing a plug, to reinforce a seam, and choosing a safe repair.","Visitors could learn to test a plug, reinforce a seam, and choose a safe repair.","Visitors could learn to test a plug, a seam was reinforced, and choosing a safe repair.","Visitors could learn a plug test, reinforcing a seam, and a safe repair choice."],"a":1,"why":"The three coordinated actions use the same verb pattern."},{"n":7,"q":"In the passage, ‘economically’ most nearly means:","c":["in a way that saves or reasonably uses money","in a way that is popular with neighbors","in a way that avoids all tools","in a way that is environmentally harmful"],"a":0,"why":"The phrase contrasts repairs that are safe and affordable with those that cost too much."},{"n":8,"q":"Which claim is best supported by the repair-cafe passage?","c":["The cafe has eliminated household waste in the neighborhood.","Some visitors gained practical skills while receiving help with repairs.","All items brought to the cafe were repaired on site.","The cafe costs less than every professional repair service."],"a":1,"why":"The passage gives examples of skill-sharing and reports repairs made on site or at home."},{"n":9,"q":"Which detail best explains why the organizers plan a follow-up survey?","c":["They want to learn whether repairs lasted beyond the visit.","They want to stop accepting broken items.","They need to prove every visitor planted seeds.","They want to replace the volunteer record with guesses."],"a":0,"why":"The passage says the current record does not track how long repairs lasted."},{"n":10,"q":"Which conclusion best fits the passage?","c":["Repair cafes work only when every item can be fixed.","A repair cafe can combine practical assistance with learning, while still needing better evidence of long-term effects.","Volunteers should avoid explaining tools to visitors.","Counting visits is enough to calculate all waste prevented."],"a":1,"why":"The passage balances practical benefits with limitations in measuring long-term impact."}]}]},{"name":"Module B: Reading","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage B: Counting Urban Pollinators","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A team of volunteers began counting pollinators in three public gardens to learn whether small green spaces supported insects in a densely built neighborhood. Each week, observers spent ten minutes at the same marked area and recorded the insects they could identify. They visited in the morning when weather permitted, and they noted wind, cloud cover, and recent rain because these conditions can affect insect activity."},{"t":"p","x":"The first month produced uneven results. The garden beside a busy road had fewer recorded visits than the garden behind a school, but the team did not conclude that traffic alone explained the difference. The school garden had more flowering plants, and its sheltered corners stayed warmer on cool mornings. A third garden, located on a rooftop, attracted fewer species overall but included several pollinators that the other sites rarely recorded."},{"t":"p","x":"The volunteers used the counts to guide a small change rather than to rank the gardens. They added a range of native flowers to the roadside garden and left a strip of bare soil for ground-nesting insects. They also asked the school to stagger mowing so that some flowers would remain available later in the season. During the next eight weeks, the roadside garden showed more visits from several common pollinator groups. The team could not determine whether the change resulted from the new plants, the weather, or both."},{"t":"p","x":"The project illustrates why repeated observations matter. A single count can reflect an unusually hot morning or a brief period when flowers are blooming. Repeated counts make it easier to notice patterns, but they do not remove every source of uncertainty. The volunteers therefore share their records with notes about missing visits and changes to the gardens."},{"t":"p","x":"The team hopes the data will help residents ask better questions: which plants attract different insects, which sites need more shade, and when maintenance might interrupt flowering. The counts are not a complete census of urban wildlife. They are a practical starting point for improving small habitats and testing whether changes make a measurable difference."},{"t":"p","x":"Follow-up count at three gardens: Roadside 42 visits (cid:127) School 57 visits (cid:127) Rooftop 31 visits."}]},"questions":[{"n":11,"q":"What is the central idea of the pollinator passage?","c":["One garden always attracts more insects than every other garden.","Repeated local observations can guide habitat improvements, but the data require cautious interpretation.","Pollinators avoid gardens beside roads under all conditions.","Volunteers can identify every insect after a short training session."],"a":1,"why":"The passage describes monitoring, a targeted change, and limits on causal claims."},{"n":12,"q":"Why did observers record wind, cloud cover, and recent rain?","c":["To make every garden have identical weather","To account for conditions that can affect insect activity","To replace the insect counts with weather estimates","To show that weather never changes between sites"],"a":1,"why":"The first paragraph directly explains the purpose of recording these conditions."},{"n":13,"q":"As used in the passage, ‘uneven’ most nearly means:","c":["inconsistent or varied","perfectly balanced","impossible to measure","completely predictable"],"a":0,"why":"The results differed across sites and time, so they were not uniform."},{"n":14,"q":"Which evidence supports the claim that the roadside garden changed after the intervention?","c":["The rooftop garden was located above street level.","The volunteers used marked areas.","The roadside garden showed more visits from several common pollinator groups after new plants were added.","The team shared its records with residents."],"a":2,"why":"This is the direct before-and-after observation reported in the passage."},{"n":15,"q":"Why does the author mention that the rooftop garden attracted some species rarely seen elsewhere?","c":["To show that the gardens differed in ways that make simple rankings misleading","To prove rooftop gardens are always best","To suggest that the school garden had no flowers","To argue that counts should never be repeated"],"a":0,"why":"The detail shows that each site may support a different mix of pollinators."},{"n":16,"q":"Which tone best describes the author?","c":["Cautious and practical","Mocking and impatient","Certain that one intervention explains every result","Nostalgic and sentimental"],"a":0,"why":"The author presents useful findings but repeatedly acknowledges uncertainty."},{"n":17,"q":"A follow-up count records 42 visits at the roadside garden, 57 at the school garden, and 31 at the rooftop garden. Which conclusion follows directly?","c":["The school garden recorded the most visits in that count.","The school garden always has the most pollinators.","The rooftop garden has no ecological value.","Traffic caused every difference among the gardens."],"a":0,"why":"The values support a comparison for this count only."},{"n":18,"q":"What does the passage suggest about a single pollinator count?","c":["It always reveals a permanent trend.","It can be affected by temporary conditions and should not be treated as conclusive by itself.","It is more reliable than repeated counts.","It proves which plant caused an insect to arrive."],"a":1,"why":"The passage notes that weather and flowering periods can affect a single count."},{"n":19,"q":"Which method would most strengthen the team’s evaluation of the flower additions?","c":["Stop recording weather conditions","Compare repeated counts over time and document changes at each site","Use only the highest count from each garden","Assume all gardens began with identical plant cover"],"a":1,"why":"Repeated observations and records of site changes help evaluate patterns while acknowledging confounding factors."},{"n":20,"q":"Which statement best synthesizes the two passages in Practice Test 3?","c":["Community projects are useful only when they produce perfect data.","Both projects combine practical action with records that help people learn, while recognizing limits in what the records prove.","Both projects show that volunteers should replace trained professionals.","Both projects measure success only by the number of visitors."],"a":1,"why":"Both passages describe community action supported by data and careful interpretation."}]}]},{"name":"Module C: Reading And Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":null,"questions":[{"n":21,"q":"A planning memo says a new schedule may change how many people arrive at a public venue before noon. The writer is choosing between a noun and a verb. Choose the correct word: ‘The new schedule may ___ how many visitors arrive before noon.’","c":["effect","affect","effects","affected"],"a":1,"why":"Affect is the verb meaning ‘influence.’"},{"n":22,"q":"A report describes a team of volunteers who checked records before the findings were published. The missing word must refer to the people performing the action. Choose the correct relative pronoun: ‘The volunteers ___ checked the records found two missing dates.’","c":["whom","whose","who","which"],"a":2,"why":"Who serves as the subject of ‘checked’ and refers to people."},{"n":23,"q":"Several teachers donated a cabinet to a school and want the label to show that the cabinet belongs to the group, not to one teacher. Which option correctly shows that the cabinet belongs to several teachers?","c":["the teacher's cabinet","the teachers cabinet","the teachers' cabinet","the teachers's cabinet"],"a":2,"why":"A plural noun ending in s takes an apostrophe after the s to show possession."},{"n":24,"q":"A first sample produced too few responses to support a strong conclusion. The research team then increased the sample and repeated the measurement. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Choose the best transition: ‘The first sample was small. ___, the researchers repeated the measurement with a larger group.’","c":["Therefore","Similarly","For instance","Meanwhile"],"a":0,"why":"The larger measurement responds logically to the limitation of the first sample."},{"n":25,"q":"A city guide names a garden and adds information about the month it opened. The added clause is not needed to identify which garden the guide means. Which sentence correctly punctuates a nonessential clause?","c":["The garden which opened in May, now attracts families.","The garden, which opened in May, now attracts families.","The garden, which opened in May now attracts families.","The garden which opened, in May now attracts families."],"a":1,"why":"A nonessential clause needs commas on both sides."},{"n":26,"q":"A museum guide reports what a visitor asked about opening hours. The writer must keep indirect-question word order in the reported question. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Which sentence correctly reports a question indirectly?","c":["The guide asked whether the room was open.","The guide asked, whether was the room open.","The guide asked whether was the room open?","The guide asked whether, the room was open."],"a":0,"why":"Indirect questions use statement word order and usually do not take a question mark unless the whole sentence is a direct question."},{"n":27,"q":"An editor notices that a sentence begins with an introductory phrase describing a person who performed an action. The current subject names the result instead of that person. Which revision fixes the dangling modifier? ‘After reviewing the chart, the error became obvious.’","c":["After reviewing the chart, the error became obvious.","After reviewing the chart, Maya noticed the error.","The error, after reviewing the chart, became obvious.","Reviewing the chart, there was an obvious error."],"a":1,"why":"Maya is the person who reviewed the chart, so the modifier attaches to the correct subject."},{"n":28,"q":"A research summary will be shared with a school board. The writer wants the sentence to sound precise and professional rather than casual or vague. Which sentence maintains a formal, precise tone?","c":["The survey was kind of a big deal for the neighborhood.","The survey provided useful evidence about residents’ travel patterns.","The survey was super cool and told us stuff.","The survey got a bunch of things going."],"a":1,"why":"The wording is specific and appropriately formal."},{"n":29,"q":"A report describes a short trial and the team's response to the result. The editor wants two complete, related thoughts joined correctly. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","c":["The trial ended early, the team reviewed the data.","The trial ended early; the team reviewed the data.","The trial ended; early the team reviewed the data.","The trial; ended early the team reviewed the data."],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon joins two independent clauses."},{"n":30,"q":"One report describes shaded benches near bus stops, while another describes signs showing arrival times. A planner wants one sentence that combines the two benefits. Text A says a town installed shaded benches near bus stops. Text B says the town also added clear signs showing bus arrival times. Which synthesis is best?","c":["The town improved waiting areas through both physical comfort and clearer information.","The signs replaced the need for bus service.","The benches reduced all delays.","Both texts argue that passengers should avoid buses."],"a":0,"why":"The synthesis combines the distinct benefits described in the two texts."}]}]}]},"s22-practice-test-4":{"session":22,"title":"Practice Test 4 (full-length mock)","intro":"A full-length, original 85-question mock: Writing 35, Reading 25, Reading and Writing 25. Allow 90 minutes in total, with 30 minutes per module, following the official 2026 module timing.","perModule":30,"modules":[{"name":"Module 1: Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage A: A Seed-Exchange Cabinet","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The city of Marlow opened a seed-exchange cabinet outside its public library after residents asked for a low-cost way to share plants suited to local conditions. Gardeners can leave labeled packets of saved seeds and take packets donated by others. Each packet lists the plant, the approximate date collected, and any growing notes the donor can provide. The library does not certify every seed, so it asks visitors to treat labels as useful information rather than a guarantee of a particular harvest."},{"t":"p","x":"At first, the cabinet held mostly popular vegetables. Within weeks, residents began adding native flowers, herbs, and seeds from plants that survived the neighborhood's dry summers. A local gardening group offered short demonstrations on storing seeds and avoiding accidental cross-pollination. The group also explained why some plants do not produce seeds that grow true to the parent variety. These sessions helped visitors understand that exchanging seeds requires more than placing packets on a shelf."},{"t":"p","x":"The library records how many packets arrive and leave, but the counts reveal only part of the program's effect. One packet may contain a few seeds; another may contain enough for an entire shared plot. Some visitors take packets without planting them immediately, while others return months later to donate seeds from successful plants. Staff therefore added a short voluntary survey asking whether visitors planted the seeds, what grew, and what they learned."},{"t":"p","x":"After one year, the cabinet had circulated 1,240 packets. Survey responses suggested that many users tried a plant they had never grown before, and several community gardens reported exchanging seeds with nearby schools. The survey reached only a portion of visitors, so the library avoids claiming that the cabinet changed gardening habits across the whole city. Instead, it plans to compare future packet counts with survey responses and to ask whether new participants feel confident saving seeds of their own."},{"t":"p","x":"The cabinet works best as a shared learning space, not simply as a free supply shelf. Its long-term value depends on accurate labels, thoughtful stewardship, and a community willing to share both successes and failures."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Which revision most concisely expresses the opening idea of Passage A?","c":["Marlow opened a library seed-exchange cabinet after residents requested a low-cost way to share locally suitable plants.","Marlow opened a cabinet at the library after residents made a request for a way in which plants could be shared at low cost.","The opening of a seed cabinet happened because residents had asked for something that would allow them to share plants.","Residents requested, and then the city opened, a cabinet that was for the exchange of seeds of plants."],"a":0,"why":"A preserves the key details without unnecessary phrases."},{"n":2,"q":"Which word best completes the sentence? ‘The library does not ___ every seed, so packet labels are not guarantees.’","c":["certify","certify about","certify to","certify with"],"a":0,"why":"Certify takes a direct object; the sentence already supplies it."},{"n":3,"q":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","c":["The number of packets have increased each month.","The number of packets has increased each month.","The number of packets are increasing each month.","The number of packets were increased each month."],"a":1,"why":"The subject is the singular phrase ‘the number,’ so ‘has’ agrees."},{"n":4,"q":"Which punctuation correctly follows the introductory phrase? ‘After the first season the gardening group offered demonstrations.’","c":["After the first season, the gardening group offered demonstrations.","After, the first season the gardening group offered demonstrations.","After the first season; the gardening group offered demonstrations.","After the first season: the gardening group offered demonstrations."],"a":0,"why":"A comma follows the introductory phrase."},{"n":5,"q":"Which transition best completes the sentence? ‘The packet count rose quickly. ___, staff could not tell how many packets were actually planted.’","c":["Therefore","For example","Nevertheless","Likewise"],"a":2,"why":"The second sentence contrasts high circulation with limited evidence about planting."},{"n":6,"q":"Which revision correctly combines the sentences? ‘The cabinet circulated 1,240 packets. The survey reached only a portion of visitors.’","c":["The cabinet circulated 1,240 packets, the survey reached only a portion of visitors.","The cabinet circulated 1,240 packets; the survey reached only a portion of visitors.","The cabinet circulated 1,240 packets, reaching the survey only a portion of visitors.","The cabinet circulated 1,240 packets; and, the survey reached only a portion of visitors."],"a":1,"why":"The semicolon correctly joins two independent clauses."},{"n":7,"q":"Which option maintains parallel structure? ‘The group taught visitors to store seeds, how to label packets, and avoiding cross-pollination.’","c":["to store seeds, labeling packets, and to avoid cross-pollination","seed storage, how packets are labeled, and avoiding cross-pollination","to store seeds, label packets, and avoid cross-pollination","storing seeds, label packets, and avoiding cross-pollination"],"a":2,"why":"The three actions follow a consistent verb pattern."},{"n":8,"q":"Why does the author mention that some plants do not produce seeds true to the parent variety?","c":["To show that seed exchange requires knowledge as well as sharing","To argue that all donated seeds are unusable","To prove that the library should sell seeds","To suggest that native plants cannot grow in Marlow"],"a":0,"why":"The detail explains why the demonstrations teach practical limits and methods."},{"n":9,"q":"Which revision removes redundancy? ‘The library plans to compare future packet counts with survey responses in the future.’","c":["The library plans to compare future packet counts with survey responses.","The library plans to compare packet counts in the future with future survey responses in the future.","The library plans to compare together future packet counts with survey responses.","The library has a plan to make a comparison of future packet counts with responses later in the future."],"a":0,"why":"Removing the final ‘in the future’ avoids repeating the same time reference."},{"n":10,"q":"Which sentence correctly punctuates a list after a complete clause?","c":["The cabinet accepts: vegetables, herbs, and native flowers.","The cabinet accepts vegetables, herbs, and native flowers.","The cabinet accepts; vegetables, herbs, and native flowers.","The cabinet accepts, vegetables herbs, and native flowers."],"a":1,"why":"A colon should not separate a verb from its direct objects in this construction."},{"n":11,"q":"Which pronoun correctly completes the sentence? ‘Each visitor may record ___ results on the voluntary survey.’","c":["their","his or her own","them","theirs own"],"a":1,"why":"‘Each visitor’ is grammatically singular in this formal sentence; ‘his or her own’ agrees with it."},{"n":12,"q":"Which sentence would best conclude Passage A?","c":["The cabinet’s value depends on accurate information and continued community participation.","The library also has a large collection of novels and biographies.","Most gardens require sunlight during the day.","The city should stop collecting information about seed packets."],"a":0,"why":"The conclusion restates the passage’s focus on shared learning, accurate labels, and stewardship."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage B: Rethinking Museum Labels","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A small museum redesigned several exhibit labels after visitors said that the displays contained interesting objects but offered too little guidance about why they mattered. The old labels often began with dates and names, then presented a dense paragraph of facts. The new labels start with a question or a short claim, provide two or three pieces of context, and invite visitors to look closely at a feature of the object. A longer text remains available for people who want more detail."},{"t":"p","x":"The design team tested two versions of a label beside the same ceramic bowl. One version began with the object's age and place of origin. The other asked visitors to notice a repair along the rim before explaining how the bowl had been used and mended. During scheduled observation periods, more visitors stopped to examine the bowl when the label directed their attention to the repair. The team also interviewed a small number of visitors, some of whom said the question made them feel comfortable looking carefully even when they knew little about ceramics."},{"t":"p","x":"The test does not establish that question-led labels will work equally well for every object. The bowl had a visible repair that naturally invited close inspection, while a landscape painting or a small coin may require a different approach. The observations also took place on a limited number of days, and visitors who agreed to interviews may not represent everyone who entered the museum."},{"t":"p","x":"The museum plans to test revised labels in several galleries. Staff will record how long visitors spend near selected objects, but they will not treat longer viewing time as proof of deeper understanding. They will also ask visitors to describe one idea they remember, giving the team a second kind of evidence. If the two measures point in different directions, the staff will examine the labels rather than assuming one measure is correct."},{"t":"p","x":"The redesign aims to make exhibits more inviting without oversimplifying the objects. A good label should open a path into the evidence, not tell visitors what they must think. By testing different approaches and acknowledging the limits of its observations, the museum hopes to make interpretation clearer while preserving room for curiosity."}]},"questions":[{"n":13,"q":"Which verb tense best completes the sentence describing the current label design? ‘The new labels ___ a question, then provide context and invite close observation.’","c":["began with","begin with","had begun with","will have begun with"],"a":1,"why":"The present tense describes the museum’s current procedure."},{"n":14,"q":"In Passage B, ‘interpretation’ most nearly refers to:","c":["the process of explaining the meaning of an object","the act of repairing a broken object","the method of measuring a room’s temperature","the choice to remove all historical details"],"a":0,"why":"The passage discusses labels that help visitors understand why objects matter."},{"n":15,"q":"Which punctuation correctly sets off the nonessential clause? ‘The bowl which had a visible repair invited close inspection.’","c":["The bowl, which had a visible repair, invited close inspection.","The bowl which, had a visible repair invited close inspection.","The bowl, which had a visible repair invited close inspection.","The bowl which had a visible repair, invited close inspection."],"a":0,"why":"A nonessential clause needs commas before and after it."},{"n":16,"q":"Choose the sentence without a comma splice.","c":["The label asked a question, more visitors stopped to look.","The label asked a question; more visitors stopped to look.","The label asked a question, and, more visitors stopped to look.","The label asked a question; because more visitors stopped to look."],"a":1,"why":"The semicolon joins two complete, related sentences."},{"n":17,"q":"Which transition best links the positive observation to the limitation? ‘More visitors stopped to examine the bowl. ___, the test does not establish that question-led labels work for every object.’","c":["For example","Consequently","Still","Similarly"],"a":2,"why":"Still introduces a qualification to the encouraging result."},{"n":18,"q":"Why does the author describe the two versions of the bowl label?","c":["To illustrate how the team tested different approaches to directing attention","To prove that every museum object needs a question","To show that visitors dislike dates","To argue that longer labels always work better"],"a":0,"why":"The comparison gives a concrete example of the label experiment."},{"n":19,"q":"Which revision is most parallel? ‘Staff will record viewing time, asking visitors what they remember, and the comparison of responses.’","c":["viewing time, asking visitors what they remember, and comparing responses","viewing time, visitor recall, and comparisons of responses","recording viewing time, ask visitors what they remember, and comparisons","to record viewing time, asking visitors what they remember, and compare responses"],"a":1,"why":"All three items are noun phrases: viewing time, visitor recall, and comparisons."},{"n":20,"q":"Which opening sentence best introduces Passage B?","c":["A museum revised its exhibit labels to help visitors understand the significance of displayed objects.","The museum’s café serves tea and coffee near the entrance.","Some visitors prefer to arrive early on weekends.","Ceramic bowls are made from clay."],"a":0,"why":"It introduces the passage’s central problem and response."},{"n":21,"q":"Which choice correctly completes the sentence? ‘The team interviewed visitors, many of ___ said the question encouraged them to look closely.’","c":["who","whom","whose","which"],"a":1,"why":"‘Whom’ is the object of ‘of’ in the phrase ‘many of whom.’"},{"n":22,"q":"Which option correctly shows possession by the museum’s staff?","c":["the museums staff","the museum's staff","the museums' staff","the museum staff's"],"a":1,"why":"The singular noun ‘museum’ takes ’s to show possession."},{"n":23,"q":"Which revision is clearest and most concise? ‘The observations took place on a limited number of days, and this means that the observations may not represent all visitors.’","c":["Because observations occurred on few days, they may not represent all visitors.","The observations took place on a limited number of days, and this means that the observations may not represent all visitors.","Observations happened a limited number of days and this fact is something that means all visitors may not be represented.","The observations, which were observations on a limited number of days, may not represent all visitors because of that."],"a":0,"why":"A removes repetition while preserving the limitation."},{"n":24,"q":"Which statement best captures the author’s main point?","c":["Museums should replace all labels with questions.","Testing different label designs can improve clarity, but staff should evaluate more than one kind of evidence.","Longer viewing time always proves learning.","Visitors should not be invited to interpret objects."],"a":1,"why":"The passage supports experimentation while warning against equating viewing time with understanding."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage C: A School Energy Dashboard","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A secondary school installed an energy dashboard that displays electricity use in the science wing, library, and sports hall. The project began after students noticed that lights and ventilation systems sometimes remained on in empty rooms. Rather than asking each class to rely on memory, the facilities team wanted a shared record that could help people identify patterns and test changes."},{"t":"p","x":"The dashboard shows hourly use alongside the school's timetable. Students can compare a normal school day with a weekend, or examine whether energy use falls after a room closes. In the first month, the sports hall used more electricity on evenings when two activities overlapped. The science wing showed a smaller decline than expected after classes ended, which led staff to check whether ventilation settings matched the building's schedule. The team found one system running longer than necessary and adjusted its timer."},{"t":"p","x":"The dashboard does not measure every environmental cost of the school. It records electricity, not the energy used to manufacture equipment or the emissions associated with every source of power. Nor can a high reading automatically identify a wasteful action; laboratories may need ventilation for safety, and a crowded hall requires more lighting than an empty one. Students must therefore interpret the numbers alongside information about what was happening in each space."},{"t":"p","x":"After the timer adjustment, average evening electricity use in the science wing fell by 12 percent over four comparable weeks. The facilities team considers this a promising result, not a final estimate of annual savings. Outdoor temperature, special events, and changes in room use could also affect consumption. Staff will continue monitoring the wing and compare similar periods before calculating a longer-term figure."},{"t":"p","x":"Teachers now use the dashboard in mathematics and science lessons. Students graph changes, explain possible causes, and identify what additional information would make a claim stronger. The project has turned a building system into a shared investigation: the numbers matter, but so do the questions and the limits attached to them."}]},"questions":[{"n":25,"q":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","c":["The dashboard, along with the timetable, help students interpret electricity use.","The dashboard, along with the timetable, helps students interpret electricity use.","The dashboard, along with the timetable, have helped students interpret electricity use.","The dashboard, along with the timetable, were helping students interpret electricity use."],"a":1,"why":"The subject is singular ‘dashboard’; the phrase ‘along with the timetable’ does not change agreement."},{"n":26,"q":"Which punctuation best introduces the examples? ‘Students can compare several periods ___ a normal school day, a weekend, or the hours after classes end.’","c":[",",";",":","?"],"a":2,"why":"A colon follows the complete clause and introduces the examples."},{"n":27,"q":"Which word best completes the sentence? ‘The hall needs ___ electricity when it is empty than when two activities overlap.’","c":["less","fewer","fewest","many"],"a":0,"why":"Electricity is uncountable in this context, so ‘less’ is correct."},{"n":28,"q":"Which transition best completes the sentence? ‘The science wing’s evening use fell by 12 percent. ___, staff will continue monitoring before estimating annual savings.’","c":["For example","Nevertheless","Similarly","In contrast"],"a":1,"why":"The continued monitoring qualifies the encouraging result."},{"n":29,"q":"Which revision fixes the dangling modifier? ‘After reviewing the dashboard, a timer was adjusted by the facilities team.’","c":["After reviewing the dashboard, a timer was adjusted by the facilities team.","After reviewing the dashboard, the facilities team adjusted a timer.","The timer, after reviewing the dashboard, was adjusted by the facilities team.","Reviewing the dashboard, there was a timer adjustment."],"a":1,"why":"The facilities team performed the review, so it should be the subject of the introductory modifier."},{"n":30,"q":"Which phrase best replaces ‘a shared record that could help people identify patterns and test changes’ without changing the meaning?","c":["a common record for spotting patterns and evaluating changes","a record that was shared by people and had patterns in it","a shared record to make every change successful","a record that could identify all causes without further evidence"],"a":0,"why":"It keeps the original meaning in fewer, more precise words."},{"n":31,"q":"Which sentence would best support the passage’s explanation of why students must interpret the dashboard carefully?","c":["A high reading during a laboratory session may reflect required ventilation rather than waste.","The dashboard uses a digital screen.","The sports hall contains several doors.","Students have different favorite subjects."],"a":0,"why":"It gives a relevant example of why usage data needs context."},{"n":32,"q":"Which punctuation best emphasizes the qualification in this sentence? ‘The dashboard records electricity use ___ it does not measure every environmental cost.’","c":[",",":","—","?"],"a":2,"why":"A dash can connect the main statement to a closely related qualification; a comma alone would splice independent clauses."},{"n":33,"q":"Which choice combines the sentences correctly? ‘The team adjusted the timer. Evening use then fell.’","c":["The team adjusted the timer, evening use then fell.","The team adjusted the timer; evening use then fell.","The team adjusted the timer; and, evening use then fell.","The team adjusted the timer because; evening use then fell."],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon joins the two independent clauses without a comma splice."},{"n":34,"q":"Which conclusion is justified by the four-week data?","c":["The adjustment was followed by a 12 percent decline in average evening use during the period measured.","The adjustment will save exactly 12 percent every year.","The school has eliminated all energy waste.","The dashboard proves that every high reading is unnecessary."],"a":0,"why":"It limits the claim to the measured period and avoids unsupported projections."},{"n":35,"q":"Which sentence best concludes Passage C?","c":["The dashboard helps students use data to investigate energy use while considering context and uncertainty.","Electricity is the only factor that affects the environment.","The school should stop collecting data after one month.","Every room should use the same amount of energy."],"a":0,"why":"It synthesizes the project’s educational purpose and its emphasis on careful interpretation."}]}]},{"name":"Module 2: Reading","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage A: Restoring The Fenwick Estuary","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Along the edge of the Fenwick estuary, a broad marsh once absorbed high tides before water reached nearby paths and fields. A road embankment narrowed the marsh in the 1970s, and drainage channels later redirected water away from several shallow pools. Over time, the exposed banks eroded in places, while reeds became less common in areas that no longer flooded regularly. Local officials debated whether to reinforce the shore with a continuous concrete wall or restore some of the marsh's tidal connections."},{"t":"p","x":"The restoration team chose a limited trial. Engineers reopened two channels and removed debris from a third, allowing water to reach low ground during ordinary tides. Volunteers planted native reeds in a few bare patches, but most areas were left to recolonize naturally. Researchers marked permanent plots and measured water depth, plant cover, sediment movement, and the number of small fish observed during scheduled surveys."},{"t":"p","x":"The first season produced uneven results. Several planted reeds died during a dry spell, and one channel filled with sediment faster than expected. At the same time, water reached shallow pools that had remained isolated for years. By the end of the second season, plant cover had increased at most plots, and fish counts in the reopened channels exceeded those in a nearby untreated section on several survey dates. The team avoided treating those observations as proof that the channels alone caused the differences; rainfall, temperature, and seasonal movement could also influence the results."},{"t":"p","x":"Marsh vegetation may slow moving water and hold sediment with its roots, but the trial has not yet experienced a storm severe enough to show how much protection the restored area can provide. The researchers are comparing the trial site with other shoreline sections and recording conditions through different seasons. They are also tracking maintenance needs, because a channel that requires frequent clearing may have different long-term costs from one that remains open naturally."},{"t":"p","x":"The team also compared photographs taken from fixed points along the bank. Those images helped staff distinguish a temporary change after a high tide from a gradual shift in the shoreline. The photographs did not replace measurements of sediment or water depth, but they gave residents a visible record of what the instruments were tracking."},{"t":"p","x":"Residents have joined monitoring walks and asked why officials have delayed the concrete-wall decision. The project has not established that natural restoration can replace engineered protection everywhere. It has provided evidence that a less rigid option deserves careful testing before the town commits to a costly permanent design."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of the Fenwick estuary passage?","c":["A limited marsh-restoration trial provides useful evidence, but more monitoring is needed before officials choose a permanent shoreline plan.","The marsh has already proved that concrete protection is never necessary.","All planted reeds survived the first season.","Residents oppose every form of shoreline protection."],"a":0,"why":"The passage describes a trial, mixed findings, limits, and a delayed decision."},{"n":37,"q":"Why did the team reopen channels and remove debris?","c":["To allow tidal water to reach shallow areas that had become isolated","To prevent all sediment from moving","To replace every native plant with a new species","To create a continuous concrete wall"],"a":0,"why":"The restoration aimed to reconnect parts of the marsh with tidal movement."},{"n":38,"q":"What can reasonably be inferred from the team's decision to leave most areas to recolonize naturally?","c":["The team wanted to observe how vegetation returned without planting every area itself.","The team believed plants could never grow in marshes.","The team had no way to record plant cover.","The team expected every seedling to survive."],"a":0,"why":"Leaving areas unplanted permits observation of natural recovery alongside the limited planting."},{"n":39,"q":"As used in the passage, ‘recolonize’ most nearly means:","c":["return to and establish a presence in an area","remove all existing plants","permanently abandon a place","cover an area with concrete"],"a":0,"why":"The passage describes vegetation becoming established again in bare areas."},{"n":40,"q":"How is the passage mainly organized?","c":["It presents a problem, describes a trial, reports mixed evidence, and explains next steps.","It lists unrelated facts about several estuaries.","It begins with a conclusion and then rejects all supporting evidence.","It compares two fictional characters."],"a":0,"why":"The passage follows the restoration project from context through results and evaluation."},{"n":41,"q":"Which evidence most directly indicates that tidal connections changed local conditions?","c":["Water reached shallow pools that had remained isolated for years.","Residents attended monitoring walks.","Officials debated two possible plans.","The project required a limited trial."],"a":0,"why":"This observation records a direct physical change after the channels reopened."},{"n":42,"q":"Which tone best describes the passage?","c":["Measured and evidence-focused","Triumphant and absolute","Sarcastic and dismissive","Fearful and speculative"],"a":0,"why":"The author reports encouraging observations while acknowledging uncertainty."},{"n":43,"q":"Why does the author mention that several planted reeds died during a dry spell?","c":["To show that the first season had setbacks as well as positive observations","To prove that reeds cannot survive in marshes","To suggest that the team stopped monitoring","To establish that rainfall never affects plants"],"a":0,"why":"The detail contributes to the mixed-results account and prevents an overly positive summary."},{"n":44,"q":"A survey recorded plant cover at three plots: Plot A rose from 22% to 35%; Plot B rose from 18% to 25%; Plot C fell from 30% to 27%. Which statement is supported?","c":["All three plots increased in plant cover.","Plot A had the largest increase in plant cover.","Plot C had the largest increase.","The survey proves the channels caused every change."],"a":1,"why":"Plot A increased by 13 percentage points, more than Plot B; Plot C declined."},{"n":45,"q":"What limitation does the author identify about the fish counts?","c":["Seasonal and environmental factors could also influence the observed differences.","Researchers never counted fish.","Fish cannot be observed in channels.","The untreated section was removed before comparison."],"a":0,"why":"The passage explicitly cautions that rainfall, temperature, and seasonal movement may affect results."},{"n":46,"q":"Why does the passage discuss maintenance needs?","c":["To show that long-term cost and upkeep matter when comparing restoration with engineered protection","To argue that researchers should ignore costs","To prove that every channel must be cleared daily","To shift the topic to public transport"],"a":0,"why":"The project must be evaluated for both ecological outcomes and maintenance demands."},{"n":47,"q":"What is the most reasonable prediction about the next phase of the project?","c":["Researchers will continue comparing sites and collecting seasonal evidence before making broader claims.","Officials will immediately remove all shoreline structures.","The team will stop recording water depth.","The town will assume that one trial applies to every coast."],"a":0,"why":"The passage repeatedly emphasizes continued monitoring and cautious decisions."},{"n":48,"q":"Which statement best summarizes the author's position?","c":["Natural restoration deserves evaluation as one possible approach, but the current evidence does not settle every shoreline decision.","Concrete walls always fail.","A single season proves the final outcome of restoration.","Residents should not participate in monitoring."],"a":0,"why":"This captures both the potential value and the stated limits of the trial."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage B: Testing A Night-Bus Route","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"When the city of Northbridge reduced late-evening bus service, officials expected most affected passengers to shift their travel times. Complaints from hospital staff, restaurant workers, and students suggested that the change had created a different problem: some trips ended before the last bus arrived, leaving passengers to pay for a taxi or wait for a family member. The transport department therefore tested a limited night route on three corridors rather than restoring every former service immediately."},{"t":"p","x":"The trial used smaller buses and ran at thirty-minute intervals between 9:30 p.m. and midnight. Planners selected the corridors using passenger counts, shift-work schedules, and the locations of major transfer points. They also placed a short survey on buses and at two stations. The survey asked where passengers were traveling from, whether the route replaced another form of transport, and how often they expected to use it. Responses were voluntary, so the results could not represent every potential rider."},{"t":"p","x":"During the first eight weeks, the route carried an average of 68 passengers per night. Use varied considerably: Friday and Saturday were busiest, while rainy weekdays were often quieter. A hospital stop accounted for a substantial share of boardings after 10 p.m. The figures suggested that demand was concentrated at particular times and locations, but the department did not assume that every passenger would otherwise have used a car or taxi. Some might have changed shifts, shared rides, or not traveled at all."},{"t":"p","x":"The trial also revealed an operational challenge. Several buses arrived late when the route crossed a congested central intersection. Planners adjusted one departure time and added a connection window at the main station. The next report will compare punctuality, passenger counts, operating cost, and survey responses before recommending whether to extend the service. A high passenger count alone will not settle the decision; officials must also consider who benefits, how reliably the buses run, and what the service costs per trip."},{"t":"p","x":"Planners also compared the trial timetable with the schedules of the largest employers along the route. That comparison suggested that a small adjustment to one departure might help some shift workers without requiring another bus. The department has not adopted the change permanently; it will test the revised timetable and compare late arrivals across several more weeks."},{"t":"p","x":"The pilot is best understood as a way to gather evidence about a specific need. It cannot answer every question about night transport, but it can show which routes and time windows deserve closer attention."}]},"questions":[{"n":49,"q":"What is the main purpose of Northbridge's night-route trial?","c":["To gather evidence about whether limited late-evening service meets a specific travel need","To restore every former bus route immediately","To replace hospital shifts with daytime work","To prove that all passengers prefer small buses"],"a":0,"why":"The trial tests three corridors before officials decide whether to expand service."},{"n":50,"q":"Which groups helped reveal the problem created by the service reduction?","c":["Hospital staff, restaurant workers, and students","Only bus drivers","Museum visitors and gardeners","Researchers studying marsh vegetation"],"a":0,"why":"The opening paragraph names these groups as sources of complaints."},{"n":51,"q":"As used in the passage, ‘corridors’ most nearly refers to:","c":["planned travel routes or pathways","enclosed rooms in a hospital","survey questions","bus maintenance tools"],"a":0,"why":"The department tested the service on three defined routes."},{"n":52,"q":"Why were the survey responses treated cautiously?","c":["Participation was voluntary, so respondents might not represent all potential riders.","The survey asked no questions about travel.","Every passenger refused to answer.","The survey was conducted before the trial began."],"a":0,"why":"Voluntary responses can introduce selection bias and do not represent everyone automatically."},{"n":53,"q":"What can be inferred from the hospital stop's share of late-night boardings?","c":["The route may be particularly useful for some workers whose schedules extend late into the evening.","Every hospital employee uses the bus.","The route is unnecessary on weekends.","The hospital controls all bus departures."],"a":0,"why":"The concentration of boardings suggests a relevant use case without proving every worker's behavior."},{"n":54,"q":"Which evidence would best help officials judge whether the route operates reliably?","c":["Punctuality records showing actual arrival times compared with scheduled times","The color of the buses","The number of posters at the station","The names of passengers who ride on Fridays"],"a":0,"why":"Punctuality records directly measure reliability."},{"n":55,"q":"Why does the author mention the departure-time adjustment and connection window?","c":["To show that the team used early evidence to make an operational change","To prove congestion no longer occurs in the city","To argue that surveys are unnecessary","To show that the route ended after eight weeks"],"a":0,"why":"The changes illustrate how the pilot can identify and address practical problems."},{"n":56,"q":"Which tone best describes the passage?","c":["Practical and cautious","Angry and accusatory","Certain that the route should expand immediately","Comic and exaggerated"],"a":0,"why":"The author focuses on measured results, operational issues, and criteria for a decision."},{"n":57,"q":"The route carried 68 passengers per night on average. Friday and Saturday were busiest, and rainy weekdays were quieter. Which conclusion is best supported?","c":["Demand varied by day and conditions during the trial.","Every night carried exactly 68 passengers.","Rain always prevents people from traveling.","Weekend use proves that weekday service has no value."],"a":0,"why":"The passage describes variation around an average, not a constant count or universal cause."},{"n":58,"q":"Why would passenger counts alone be insufficient to decide whether to expand the route?","c":["Officials also need to consider reliability, who benefits, and operating cost.","Passenger counts can never be measured.","A high count always proves a route is profitable.","The department has no interest in travel needs."],"a":0,"why":"The final paragraphs explicitly list additional criteria beyond ridership."},{"n":59,"q":"Which summary is most accurate?","c":["A limited night-bus pilot is collecting ridership and operational evidence to guide a decision about future service.","Northbridge restored all buses and ended data collection.","The route carries the same number of passengers every night.","Officials concluded that taxis are the only useful late-night transport."],"a":0,"why":"It captures the purpose, scope, and evidence-based nature of the trial."},{"n":60,"q":"How do the Fenwick marsh project and Northbridge bus trial differ in their main measures of success?","c":["Fenwick tracks ecological and physical changes, while Northbridge tracks passenger use, reliability, and operating costs.","Both projects measure only visitor satisfaction.","Fenwick studies bus punctuality, while Northbridge measures plant cover.","Neither project uses repeated observations."],"a":0,"why":"The passages describe different goals and corresponding forms of evidence."}]}]},{"name":"Module 3: Reading And Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":null,"questions":[{"n":61,"q":"A teacher is editing a sentence in a report about the school's annual survey. The three descriptions should follow the same grammatical pattern. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. The editor wrote: ‘The report is concise, clear, and it explains the results.’ Which revision is most parallel?","c":["The report is concise, clear, and explanatory.","The report is concise, clear, and it explains the results.","The report is concision, clear, and explanatory.","The report concisely, clearly, and explains results."],"a":0,"why":"The three coordinated adjectives follow a parallel structure."},{"n":62,"q":"A notice will be placed near the west gallery entrance. The curator wants to describe its possible influence on visitor numbers. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Choose the correct word: ‘The new sign may ___ the number of visitors who enter the west gallery.’","c":["effect","affect","effects","affection"],"a":1,"why":"Affect is the verb meaning influence."},{"n":63,"q":"After reviewing an early draft, a committee decided that the recommendation needed to be clearer. The writer added a sentence describing the next action. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. The committee reviewed the draft. ___, it asked the writer to clarify the final recommendation.","c":["As a result","For instance","Likewise","In contrast"],"a":0,"why":"The request follows from the review, so a result transition fits."},{"n":64,"q":"In a project summary, the team wants to name three factors that it recorded during the trial. The sentence already introduces the list. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Choose the sentence with correct punctuation.","c":["The team recorded three factors: cost, time, and safety.","The team recorded: three factors cost, time, and safety.","The team recorded three factors;: cost, time, and safety.","The team recorded three factors, cost; time and safety."],"a":0,"why":"A colon follows a complete clause and introduces the list."},{"n":65,"q":"The observatory recently reopened after repairs. A writer wants to specify that the roof belongs to the observatory. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. ‘The observatory, ___ roof was repaired last spring, has reopened.’","c":["who","whom","whose","which's"],"a":2,"why":"Whose shows possession and refers to the observatory's roof."},{"n":66,"q":"A small study compared task completion before and after practice among a limited group of participants. The researchers did not test every kind of task. A study found that a small group of participants completed a task faster after practice. Which conclusion is safest?","c":["Practice may be associated with faster completion in the group studied.","Practice guarantees that every person will become faster.","The study proves that age never affects performance.","The result applies to all tasks in every setting."],"a":0,"why":"The conclusion stays within the evidence and avoids universal claims."},{"n":67,"q":"A report explains that the first sample was too small to answer the question. The team repeated the trial with a larger group. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Choose the correct sentence boundary.","c":["The sample was small, the team repeated the trial.","The sample was small; the team repeated the trial.","The sample was small; because the team repeated the trial.","The sample was small the team; repeated the trial."],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon joins two independent clauses."},{"n":68,"q":"An analyst checked a chart before writing a short report. The draft accidentally made the error sound as though it had reviewed the chart. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. ‘After checking the figures, the error was easy to find.’ Which revision fixes the modifier?","c":["After checking the figures, the analyst found the error easily.","After checking the figures, the error was easy to find.","The error, after checking the figures, was easy to find.","Checking the figures, there was an easy error to find."],"a":0,"why":"The analyst is the person who checks the figures, so the subject must name that person."},{"n":69,"q":"A school collected notes written by several researchers. The editor needs to indicate that the notes belong to the researchers, not one person. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Which option correctly shows possession by several researchers?","c":["the researchers notes","the researcher's notes","the researchers' notes","the researchers's notes"],"a":2,"why":"The plural noun ending in s takes an apostrophe after the s."},{"n":70,"q":"A team compared two readings, but the measurements were taken under different weather conditions. The analyst wants to avoid attributing a difference to the equipment too quickly. The first reading was taken during heavy rain; the second was taken on a dry day. What should a careful analyst do before attributing a difference to the equipment?","c":["Consider weather as a possible factor affecting the readings.","Assume the equipment caused the difference.","Discard all readings without review.","Claim that the second reading is always correct."],"a":0,"why":"The conditions differ, so weather is a possible confounding factor."},{"n":71,"q":"A delivery company wrote a sentence explaining why a shipment arrived late. The editor wants to remove a wordy phrase without changing the cause. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Choose the best revision for concision: ‘Due to the fact that the road was closed, the delivery arrived late.’","c":["Because the road was closed, the delivery arrived late.","Due to the fact of the road's closure, the delivery arrived late.","The delivery arrived late for the reason that the road was closed.","The reason the delivery arrived late was because the road was closed."],"a":0,"why":"Because states the cause directly without redundant phrasing."},{"n":72,"q":"A city guide identifies a bridge and adds a date when it opened. The date supplies extra information rather than identifying which bridge the guide means. Which sentence correctly uses a nonessential clause?","c":["The bridge, which opened in 1924, is still in use.","The bridge which opened in 1924, is still in use.","The bridge, which opened in 1924 is still in use.","The bridge which, opened in 1924 is still in use."],"a":0,"why":"The nonessential clause is enclosed by commas."},{"n":73,"q":"A planning team is preparing a summary of two reports. One describes longer library hours during exam week, and the other describes additional quiet study rooms. Text A says a school extended library hours during exam week. Text B says the school also added quiet study rooms. Which synthesis is strongest?","c":["The school expanded study access through both longer hours and additional spaces.","The quiet rooms made the library unnecessary.","Exam week was canceled.","Both texts say students should study only at home."],"a":0,"why":"It combines the two complementary changes without adding unsupported claims."},{"n":74,"q":"A schedule lists volunteers and a coordinator as possible helpers. The writer wants the verb to agree with the subject nearest to it in a neither/nor construction. Which option best completes the sentence? ‘Neither the volunteers nor the coordinator ___ available on Tuesday.’","c":["are","were","is","have been"],"a":2,"why":"With neither…nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, ‘coordinator,’ which is singular."},{"n":75,"q":"A report praises a design's low price but then notes that it breaks often and requires replacement parts. The writer needs a transition that signals contrast. Which transition best signals a contrast? ‘The first design was inexpensive. ___, it required frequent repairs.’","c":["In addition","Nevertheless","For example","Similarly"],"a":1,"why":"Nevertheless introduces an unexpected drawback."},{"n":76,"q":"A city claims that planting trees lowered the temperature of a paved square. Researchers need evidence that measures the outcome rather than the planting activity. A short report states that a city added 20 trees to a square and that the shaded area increased. Which additional evidence would best support a claim that the trees reduced surface temperature?","c":["Temperature measurements taken before and after planting under comparable conditions","A list of the tree species' common names only","A photograph of the square on one cloudy day","The number of people who attended the planting event"],"a":0,"why":"Comparable before-and-after temperature data directly address the claim."},{"n":77,"q":"A scientist compares results from two trials and writes one sentence summarizing them. The verb must agree with the plural subject. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Choose the correct verb: ‘The results of the second trial ___ consistent with the first trial.’","c":["was","is","were","has been"],"a":2,"why":"The plural subject ‘results’ takes ‘were.’"},{"n":78,"q":"A museum guide needs to ask a colleague a direct question about opening hours. The embedded clause must keep statement word order. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Which sentence uses a question mark correctly?","c":["The guide asked whether the gallery was open?","Did the guide ask whether the gallery was open?","The guide asked, whether the gallery was open?","The guide asked whether, the gallery was open?"],"a":1,"why":"The entire sentence is a direct question, so it takes a question mark; the embedded question remains in statement order."},{"n":79,"q":"An editor compares a new procedure with the previous procedure. The new version requires fewer steps, and the sentence should express the comparison accurately. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Which word best completes the sentence? ‘The new procedure is ___ than the previous one because it requires fewer steps.’","c":["efficient","more efficient","most efficient","efficiently"],"a":1,"why":"The comparative form ‘more efficient’ compares two procedures."},{"n":80,"q":"A library tracks monthly visits and wants to report a change without claiming to know its cause or predict future months. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. A graph shows that library visits rose from 420 in September to 510 in October. Which statement is supported?","c":["Visits increased by 90 between September and October.","Visits will increase by 90 every month.","October had the highest visits of every year.","The change was caused by longer hours, even though no reason is given."],"a":0,"why":"510 minus 420 equals 90; the data do not establish a cause or future pattern."},{"n":81,"q":"Two teams met to share research findings, but the original sentence uses an unclear pronoun. The revision should identify which team presented the results. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Which revision best fixes the pronoun reference? ‘When the lab team met the school team, they presented its results.’","c":["When the lab team met the school team, the lab team presented its results.","When the lab team met the school team, they presented its results.","When the lab team met the school team, it presented their results.","When the lab team met the school team, they presented them."],"a":0,"why":"Naming the intended subject removes the ambiguous pronoun ‘they.’"},{"n":82,"q":"A writer explains why an author included several neighborhood examples in an informational article. The missing connector should express purpose. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Which option best completes the sentence? ‘The author lists several examples of neighborhood projects ___ readers can see how the general principle works in practice.’","c":["so that","although","unless","meanwhile"],"a":0,"why":"So that expresses the purpose of giving examples."},{"n":83,"q":"A local archive has begun providing digital copies of fragile maps. It still asks visitors to request permission before downloading high-resolution files. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Which statement is a reasonable inference from this text? ‘The archive now provides digital copies of fragile maps. Staff still require visitors to request permission before downloading high-resolution files.’","c":["The archive seeks to improve access while retaining some control over sensitive files.","The archive has stopped preserving physical maps.","All maps are available without restrictions.","Staff believe digital copies are more fragile than paper maps."],"a":0,"why":"The two details support a balance between access and controlled use."},{"n":84,"q":"A research group published the method used in its experiment. The author wants to show that publication enabled other researchers to repeat the work. The editor wants the final wording to be clear, accurate, and easy for readers to interpret. Which revision best combines the sentences? ‘The team published its method. Other researchers could repeat the experiment.’","c":["The team published its method, other researchers could repeat the experiment.","By publishing its method, the team made it possible for other researchers to repeat the experiment.","The team published its method; because other researchers could repeat the experiment.","Publishing its method, other researchers could repeat the experiment by the team."],"a":1,"why":"The revision makes the relationship clear and avoids a comma splice or dangling modifier."},{"n":85,"q":"A researcher grew two plants under different lamps for one week. The plant under lamp A grew 4 cm, while the plant under lamp B grew 3 cm. Each condition included only one plant, so the result is preliminary. Which conclusion is most defensible?","c":["The small trial suggests a possible difference, but more plants are needed for a reliable conclusion.","Lamp A always produces more growth than lamp B.","The one-centimeter difference proves lamp A is better for every plant.","Plant growth is unrelated to lighting in every setting."],"a":0,"why":"The conclusion reflects the observed difference while acknowledging the very small sample."}]}]}]},"s19-diagnostic-mock":{"session":19,"title":"Diagnostic Mock Test","perModule":30,"intro":"A full-length, original 85-question diagnostic that follows the 2026 EST I HS Literacy structure: Writing 35, Reading 25, Reading and Writing 25. After you check your answers you will see an estimated skill map and where to revise first.","modules":[{"name":"Module 1: Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":null,"questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Choose the best revision of the sentence: “The school library extended its hours, students could now study after school.”","c":["The school library extended its hours, students could now study after school.","The school library extended its hours; students could now study after school.","The school library extended its hours students, could now study after school.","The school library extended its hours: and students could now study after school."],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon can join two closely related independent clauses."},{"n":2,"q":"Which choice most clearly combines the ideas? “Mina collected the survey responses. She analyzed them.”","c":["Mina collected the survey responses, analyzing them.","After collecting the survey responses, Mina analyzed them.","Mina collected the survey responses, she analyzed them.","Collecting the survey responses. Mina analyzed them."],"a":1,"why":"The introductory phrase correctly shows that collecting came before analyzing and avoids a comma splice."},{"n":3,"q":"Choose the most concise revision: “Due to the fact that the road was flooded, the bus arrived late.”","c":["Owing to the fact that the road was flooded, the bus arrived late.","Because the road was flooded, the bus arrived late.","The bus arrived late for the reason that the road was flooded.","The bus arrived late due to the fact of flooding on the road."],"a":1,"why":"“Because” communicates the cause directly and concisely."},{"n":4,"q":"Which choice correctly punctuates the sentence? “The experiment had one unexpected result ___ the plants grew faster in indirect light.”","c":[",",";",":","?"],"a":2,"why":"A colon introduces an explanation of the preceding independent clause."},{"n":5,"q":"Choose the correct verb: “Neither the coaches nor the captain ___ available for the interview.”","c":["are","were","is","have been"],"a":2,"why":"With neither/nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, captain, which is singular."},{"n":6,"q":"Choose the correct pronoun: “Every applicant must submit ___ portfolio by Friday.”","c":["their","his or her","them","they"],"a":1,"why":"In formal test grammar, the singular applicant takes the singular pronoun phrase “his or her.”"},{"n":7,"q":"Choose the correct verb tense: “By the time the guests arrived, the chef ___ the meal.”","c":["finishes","has finished","had finished","will finish"],"a":2,"why":"Past perfect marks the action completed before another past action."},{"n":8,"q":"Which sentence uses a modifier correctly?","c":["Walking through the museum, the paintings impressed Salma.","Walking through the museum, Salma was impressed by the paintings.","The paintings, walking through the museum, impressed Salma.","Salma was impressed, walking through the museum the paintings."],"a":1,"why":"The person doing the walking must be the subject of the introductory phrase."},{"n":9,"q":"Choose the best transition: “The first trial produced inconsistent results. ___, the researchers repeated it under controlled conditions.”","c":["For example","Nevertheless","Therefore","Similarly"],"a":2,"why":"Repeating the trial is a logical response to inconsistent results; therefore signals consequence."},{"n":10,"q":"Which choice maintains parallel structure?","c":["The program teaches students to plan, drafting, and revise.","The program teaches students planning, to draft, and revision.","The program teaches students to plan, draft, and revise.","The program teaches students to plan, drafting, and revision."],"a":2,"why":"The three verbs share the same infinitive form."},{"n":11,"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","c":["The editor checked the facts, however, the article still contained an error.","The editor checked the facts; however, the article still contained an error.","The editor checked the facts however; the article still contained an error.","The editor checked, the facts; however the article still contained an error."],"a":1,"why":"A conjunctive adverb joining independent clauses follows a semicolon and is followed by a comma."},{"n":12,"q":"Which choice best combines the sentences? “The museum opened a new wing. It displays local artists.”","c":["The museum opened a new wing, it displays local artists.","The museum opened a new wing that displays local artists.","Opening a new wing. The museum displays local artists.","The museum opened a new wing; displaying local artists."],"a":1,"why":"The relative clause clearly modifies “wing” and creates a complete sentence."},{"n":13,"q":"Choose the correct possessive form: “The ___ decision surprised the committee.”","c":["teachers","teacher's","teachers'","teachers's"],"a":1,"why":"The singular noun teacher needs apostrophe-s to show possession."},{"n":14,"q":"Choose the correct word: “The new policy may ___ attendance.”","c":["effect","affect","effects","affects"],"a":1,"why":"Affect is the verb meaning to influence; effect is usually a noun."},{"n":15,"q":"Which sentence is free of a fragment?","c":["Although the team practiced every day.","Because the schedule changed unexpectedly.","The team practiced every day, and its performance improved.","While waiting for the final results."],"a":2,"why":"This choice contains a complete independent clause joined to another complete clause."},{"n":16,"q":"Choose the most logical sentence order. (1) Finally, the team published its findings. (2) First, researchers collected water samples. (3) They then tested each sample for pollutants.","c":["1–2–3","2–3–1","3–1–2","2–1–3"],"a":1,"why":"The sequence moves from collection to testing to publication."},{"n":17,"q":"Which choice best expresses the relationship? “The route was longer than expected. It was safer than the shortcut.”","c":["The route was longer than expected, it was safer than the shortcut.","Although the route was longer than expected, it was safer than the shortcut.","The route was longer than expected because it was safer than the shortcut.","The route was longer than expected; although it was safer than the shortcut."],"a":1,"why":"Although accurately signals contrast between extra distance and greater safety."},{"n":18,"q":"Choose the correct agreement: “A collection of rare maps ___ on display.”","c":["are","were","is","have been"],"a":2,"why":"The subject is collection, singular; “of rare maps” is a prepositional phrase."},{"n":19,"q":"Which choice is the clearest revision? “The reason why the event was canceled was because it rained.”","c":["The reason why the event was canceled was due to rain.","The event was canceled because it rained.","The event's cancellation happened for the reason that it rained.","Because of the fact that it rained was why the event was canceled."],"a":1,"why":"This removes redundant wording and states the cause directly."},{"n":20,"q":"Choose the correct punctuation: “The results were promising ___ however, the sample was small.”","c":[",",";",":","—"],"a":1,"why":"A semicolon separates the two independent clauses before the conjunctive adverb."},{"n":21,"q":"Which choice uses “who” or “whom” correctly? “The scientist ___ led the project presented the results.”","c":["whom","who","whose","which"],"a":1,"why":"Who is the subject of the verb “led.”"},{"n":22,"q":"Choose the best transition: “The device is inexpensive. ___, it is easy to repair.”","c":["In contrast","Furthermore","Instead","Otherwise"],"a":1,"why":"Furthermore adds another supporting advantage."},{"n":23,"q":"Which choice correctly punctuates a nonessential clause?","c":["The Nile which flows north, passes through several countries.","The Nile, which flows north, passes through several countries.","The Nile which flows north passes, through several countries.","The Nile, which flows north passes through several countries."],"a":1,"why":"The nonessential clause is set off with commas on both sides."},{"n":24,"q":"Choose the correct form: “The committee has ___ its final recommendation.”","c":["made","make","making","makes"],"a":0,"why":"The present perfect uses has + past participle; made is the past participle."},{"n":25,"q":"Which choice best combines the sentences? “The article was well researched. It was difficult to follow.”","c":["The article was well researched, it was difficult to follow.","Although the article was well researched, it was difficult to follow.","The article was well researched because it was difficult to follow.","The article was well researched; difficult to follow."],"a":1,"why":"Although presents the contrast clearly and avoids a comma splice."},{"n":26,"q":"Choose the correct word: “The principal gave ___ tickets to the students.”","c":["complementary","complimentary","compliment","complement"],"a":1,"why":"Complimentary means provided free of charge; complementary means completing or matching something."},{"n":27,"q":"Which sentence has correct parallelism?","c":["Success depends on preparation, focus, and working carefully.","Success depends on preparing, focus, and careful work.","Success depends on preparation, focus, and careful work.","Success depends on to prepare, focusing, and careful work."],"a":2,"why":"All three items are parallel noun phrases."},{"n":28,"q":"Choose the best revision: “The report was late. This caused the meeting to be postponed.”","c":["The report was late, this caused the meeting to be postponed.","The report's lateness caused the meeting to be postponed.","The report was late; causing the meeting to be postponed.","Being late, the meeting was postponed by the report."],"a":1,"why":"The revision makes the cause-and-effect relationship direct and avoids an unclear “this.”"},{"n":29,"q":"Choose the correct punctuation: “Three skills are essential ___ planning, revising, and proofreading.”","c":[",",";",":","—,"],"a":2,"why":"A colon introduces a list after a complete clause."},{"n":30,"q":"Which sentence correctly uses a dash?","c":["The solution—after several failed attempts—finally worked.","The solution—after several failed attempts finally worked.","The solution after—several failed attempts—finally worked.","The solution, after several failed attempts—finally worked."],"a":0,"why":"A pair of dashes can set off an interrupting phrase."},{"n":31,"q":"Choose the correct verb: “Each of the proposals ___ a different solution.”","c":["offer","offers","have offered","are offering"],"a":1,"why":"Each is singular and takes a singular verb."},{"n":32,"q":"Which choice is most appropriate for a formal academic report?","c":["The results were kind of surprising.","The results were pretty wild.","The results were unexpected.","The results were super strange."],"a":2,"why":"Unexpected is precise and appropriately formal."},{"n":33,"q":"Choose the best transition: “The sample size was small. ___, the results should be interpreted cautiously.”","c":["For instance","Consequently","Likewise","Meanwhile"],"a":1,"why":"Consequently shows that cautious interpretation follows from the limited sample."},{"n":34,"q":"Which sentence correctly uses a colon?","c":["The survey asked: students to rate the service.","The survey had three goals: to measure access, satisfaction, and cost.","The survey: had three goals to measure access, satisfaction, and cost.","The survey had: three goals to measure access, satisfaction, and cost."],"a":1,"why":"A colon follows a complete clause and introduces the list."},{"n":35,"q":"Choose the correct revision for clarity: “When Huda spoke to Mariam, she was nervous.”","c":["When Huda spoke to Mariam, she was nervous.","Huda was nervous when she spoke to Mariam.","When Huda spoke to Mariam, this was nervous.","Huda spoke to Mariam, being nervous she was."],"a":1,"why":"The revision removes the ambiguous pronoun by naming the nervous person."}]}]},{"name":"Module 2: Reading","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":{"title":"Passage A: A Library That Lends More Than Books","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"When the town of Marston renovated its public library, planners initially focused on replacing worn shelves and adding more study desks. During community meetings, however, residents described a wider range of needs. Some wanted reliable internet access; others needed a quiet place to complete job applications or attend online interviews. A local repair group suggested lending basic tools, while gardeners proposed sharing seeds."},{"t":"p","x":"The library board decided to test a “borrow beyond books” program. It began with a small collection of Wi-Fi hotspots, measuring tools, and gardening kits. Each item had a clear checkout period, simple instructions, and a short survey for borrowers. The board expected the tools to be the most popular items, but the hotspots were borrowed most often, especially by residents who lacked stable home internet."},{"t":"p","x":"After six months, the library reviewed its records. The program had reached more households than expected, but the data also revealed challenges. Some items were returned late, and staff needed time to inspect and maintain equipment. Rather than ending the program, the board adjusted the checkout rules, recruited volunteers to help with maintenance, and asked borrowers which items should be added next."},{"t":"p","x":"The experience changed how the board described the library’s mission. Books remained central, but the building was also a place where residents could access services, learn practical skills, and connect with one another. The program’s success did not come from guessing what the community wanted; it came from testing a modest idea, studying the evidence, and making careful changes."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"q":"Which choice best states the central idea of Passage A?","c":["Libraries should replace most printed books with technology.","A library expanded its services by testing a community-focused lending program and improving it with evidence.","Residents preferred gardening kits over all other library resources.","Volunteers are more effective than paid library staff."],"a":1,"why":"The passage follows the program from community suggestions through a trial and evidence-based adjustments."},{"n":37,"q":"Why did the library board begin with a small collection of items?","c":["It wanted to avoid holding community meetings.","It could test the idea and learn from actual use before expanding.","It had decided that only tools should be available.","It expected the program to fail."],"a":1,"why":"A limited trial let the board evaluate demand and practical challenges before making larger decisions."},{"n":38,"q":"Which item was borrowed most often?","c":["Gardening kits","Measuring tools","Wi-Fi hotspots","Repair manuals"],"a":2,"why":"The passage explicitly reports that hotspots were borrowed most often."},{"n":39,"q":"The word “modest” in the final paragraph most nearly means:","c":["expensive","limited in scale","unusual","unpopular"],"a":1,"why":"The program began as a small, manageable trial."},{"n":40,"q":"Which statement is best supported by the passage?","c":["The board predicted demand accurately.","All borrowed items were returned on time.","The board changed procedures after reviewing evidence.","The program required no additional staff effort."],"a":2,"why":"The board adjusted checkout rules and recruited maintenance volunteers after reviewing records."},{"n":41,"q":"What role does the second paragraph play?","c":["It describes the program’s initial design and an unexpected result.","It argues that books are no longer useful.","It lists every service offered by the town.","It explains why the library was built."],"a":0,"why":"The paragraph introduces the trial’s items and notes the unexpectedly high demand for hotspots."},{"n":42,"q":"Which challenge emerged during the trial?","c":["No residents used the equipment.","The library had to maintain equipment and manage late returns.","The town refused to provide internet access.","The board could not collect any data."],"a":1,"why":"Late returns and maintenance demands are named directly. EST Session 19 | Diagnostic Mock Test Page 13"},{"n":43,"q":"The board’s response suggests that it values:","c":["fixed rules that never change.","decisions informed by evidence and feedback.","reducing the library’s role in the community.","avoiding all risks."],"a":1,"why":"The board changed rules and asked borrowers what should be added."},{"n":44,"q":"Which best describes the author’s attitude toward the program?","c":["Dismissive","Cautiously positive","Angry","Uncertain that libraries matter"],"a":1,"why":"The passage presents real challenges but emphasizes learning and community benefit."},{"n":45,"q":"What can be inferred about the residents’ needs?","c":["They were limited to borrowing books.","They included practical and digital-access needs.","They all wanted the same item.","They opposed using the library for services."],"a":1,"why":"Residents requested internet access, job-application space, tools, and seeds."},{"n":46,"q":"Which phrase best captures the lesson of the program?","c":["Expand quickly without collecting feedback","Test an idea, examine results, and adjust","Focus only on the most expensive items","Avoid lending items that require maintenance"],"a":1,"why":"The final sentence explicitly summarizes this evidence-based cycle."},{"n":47,"q":"The final paragraph primarily serves to:","c":["show how the trial reshaped the board’s understanding of the library’s role.","argue that books should be removed.","explain the technical operation of hotspots.","criticize residents for asking for new services."],"a":0,"why":"It broadens the library’s mission while keeping books central."}]},{"passage":{"title":"Passage B: The Value of Urban Trees","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Urban trees are often praised for making streets more attractive, but their value extends well beyond appearance. A tree’s shade can lower surface temperatures on hot days, while leaves can intercept some airborne particles. Trees also absorb rainwater, slowing the flow that might otherwise overwhelm drainage systems. These benefits, however, depend on the location, species, age, and health of the trees."},{"t":"p","x":"For years, some city plans counted success by the number of trees planted. That measure is easy to report, but it can be misleading. A newly planted tree may die without sufficient water or suitable soil, and a tree planted in a narrow strip may struggle to develop roots. A more informative plan tracks survival rates, canopy growth, and whether shade reaches places where people walk or wait for public transport."},{"t":"p","x":"Researchers also warn against treating tree planting as a universal solution. In dry regions, water use must be considered; in some locations, roots may interfere with pipes or sidewalks if the wrong species is selected. These concerns do not weaken the case for urban trees. Instead, they show why long-term planning matters. A smaller number of healthy, well-positioned trees may provide more lasting benefits than a larger number planted without follow-up care."}]},"questions":[{"n":48,"q":"Which choice best states the central idea of Passage B?","c":["Planting as many trees as possible always benefits a city.","Urban trees offer several benefits, but effective programs require attention to location and long-term care.","Trees mainly improve the appearance of city streets.","Cities should avoid planting trees in dry regions."],"a":1,"why":"The passage describes benefits while emphasizing planning, species choice, and survival."},{"n":49,"q":"Why can counting newly planted trees be misleading?","c":["New trees never provide shade.","The count does not show whether trees survive or provide useful canopy.","Residents dislike newly planted trees.","Tree counts are impossible to calculate."],"a":1,"why":"The passage recommends tracking survival, canopy growth, and useful shade rather than planting totals alone."},{"n":50,"q":"The word “intercept” in the first paragraph most nearly means:","c":["create","capture or catch","release","measure"],"a":1,"why":"Leaves can catch some airborne particles."},{"n":51,"q":"Which measure would best help a city evaluate the lasting success of a tree program?","c":["The number of planting ceremonies held","The number of trees purchased","Tree survival rates and canopy growth over time","The total length of the city’s streets"],"a":2,"why":"The passage explicitly recommends survival rates and canopy growth."},{"n":52,"q":"What does the author suggest about possible problems such as roots damaging sidewalks?","c":["They prove urban trees are not worthwhile.","They can be reduced by selecting suitable species and locations.","They occur only in rural areas.","They matter more than all environmental benefits."],"a":1,"why":"The passage presents them as planning concerns, not reasons to abandon trees."},{"n":53,"q":"Which statement is supported by the passage?","c":["All tree species use the same amount of water.","A healthy tree in a useful location may provide more benefit than several poorly placed trees.","New trees require no care after planting.","Canopy growth is unrelated to shade."],"a":1,"why":"The conclusion compares healthy, well-positioned trees with larger numbers planted without care."},{"n":54,"q":"What is the function of the second paragraph?","c":["It challenges a simple measure of success and proposes better indicators.","It explains how to prune every tree species.","It argues that tree planting is too expensive.","It describes the history of a particular park."],"a":0,"why":"The paragraph contrasts planting counts with survival and canopy measures."},{"n":55,"q":"Which relationship is emphasized in the passage?","c":["More trees planted always means more shade.","The benefits of trees depend partly on species, conditions, and maintenance.","Tree roots cannot affect infrastructure.","Water use is never relevant to urban planning."],"a":1,"why":"Both the opening and final paragraphs emphasize context and care."},{"n":56,"q":"The author’s tone is best described as:","c":["balanced and analytical","sarcastic and mocking","fearful and alarmist","celebratory without qualification"],"a":0,"why":"The author acknowledges benefits and limitations, then argues for informed planning."},{"n":57,"q":"Which evidence would most directly support the author’s recommendation?","c":["A record of how many people attended a planting event","A five-year study comparing tree survival and canopy coverage across neighborhoods","A photograph of a newly planted tree","A list of tree names without location data"],"a":1,"why":"Long-term survival and canopy data directly test whether the program delivers lasting benefits."},{"n":58,"q":"How does Passage B develop its argument?","c":["It presents benefits, identifies a weak success measure, and explains the need for careful planning.","It lists tree species alphabetically.","It compares two unrelated cities without drawing a conclusion.","It argues against all public spending."],"a":0,"why":"This sequence captures the structure of the passage."},{"n":59,"q":"Which conclusion is most consistent with the passage?","c":["Cities should prioritize tree care and placement, not just planting totals.","Cities should stop collecting environmental data.","Only old trees have any value.","Tree planting should be judged by appearance alone."],"a":0,"why":"The conclusion stresses lasting benefits from healthy, well-positioned trees."},{"n":60,"q":"Which assumption does the author challenge most directly?","c":["That cities can report how many trees they plant","That planting totals alone are enough to measure a tree program's success","That trees can provide shade","That tree roots grow underground"],"a":1,"why":"The second paragraph argues that planting totals can be misleading and recommends tracking survival, canopy growth, and useful shade."}]}]},{"name":"Module 3: Reading and Writing","scored":true,"blocks":[{"passage":null,"questions":[{"n":61,"q":"A student report says: “The survey had 18 responses. Most respondents preferred later library hours. This proves every student in the town wants the library to stay open later.” Which revision is most defensible?","c":["This proves every student in the town wants later hours.","The responses suggest that many surveyed students preferred later hours, though the sample may not represent everyone.","No students preferred later hours.","The survey is useless because it had responses."],"a":1,"why":"The conclusion should match the limited sample and avoid overgeneralization."},{"n":62,"q":"Choose the best transition: “The first design was cheaper to produce. ___, it broke during the first test.”","c":["For example","However","Similarly","Therefore"],"a":1,"why":"However signals contrast between low cost and poor performance."},{"n":63,"q":"A school newsletter states: “The science club meets on Tuesdays. Students may join at any time during the term.” Which inference is best supported?","c":["Membership is restricted to the first week.","The club accepts students after the term begins.","The club meets every day.","Only science teachers may attend."],"a":1,"why":"The statement that students may join at any time supports later enrollment."},{"n":64,"q":"Choose the sentence that best combines the information: “The park added lights. Evening visitors increased.”","c":["The park added lights, evening visitors increased.","After the park added lights, evening visitors increased.","The park added lights evening, visitors increased.","Adding lights. Evening visitors increased."],"a":1,"why":"The introductory clause establishes a clear time relationship and produces a complete sentence."},{"n":65,"q":"A writer wants to add a specific example after this sentence: “Several low-cost changes can reduce classroom waste.” Which sentence best supports it?","c":["Waste is a problem in many places.","For example, placing labeled paper-recycling bins near printers makes reuse easier.","Many students have different opinions.","Classrooms are used for learning."],"a":1,"why":"It gives a concrete example directly related to reducing classroom waste."},{"n":66,"q":"Choose the correct verb: “The list of required materials ___ on the course website.”","c":["appear","appears","have appeared","are appearing"],"a":1,"why":"The singular subject is list."},{"n":67,"q":"A passage says a researcher repeated an experiment three times and obtained similar results. Which conclusion is best supported?","c":["The results are guaranteed to be correct.","The repeated results provide some evidence of consistency.","The experiment proves every theory.","No further testing is needed."],"a":1,"why":"Repeated similar results suggest consistency but do not guarantee correctness."},{"n":68,"q":"Choose the most concise revision: “At this point in time, the team is currently reviewing the proposal.”","c":["At this point in time, the team is currently reviewing the proposal.","The team is reviewing the proposal.","Currently, at this point, the team is reviewing the proposal.","The team, at this point in time, currently reviews the proposal."],"a":1,"why":"The revision removes redundant time expressions."},{"n":69,"q":"A short passage states: “Although the prototype was lighter, it required more frequent charging.” What relationship does “although” signal?","c":["Cause","Contrast","Sequence","Example"],"a":1,"why":"The sentence contrasts an advantage with a drawback."},{"n":70,"q":"Choose the correct punctuation: “The project has one priority ___ improving access for new readers.”","c":[",",";",":","?"],"a":2,"why":"A colon introduces an explanation of the priority."},{"n":71,"q":"A student writes: “The article presents two explanations for the decline. It then compares evidence for each.” What is the article’s structure?","c":["It lists unrelated facts.","It introduces explanations and evaluates them comparatively.","It describes a process in chronological order only.","It states a claim without evidence."],"a":1,"why":"The second sentence explicitly says the article compares evidence for each explanation."},{"n":72,"q":"Choose the correct pronoun: “The volunteers completed the task, and the coordinator thanked ___.”","c":["they","their","them","theirs"],"a":2,"why":"The object of thanked requires the object pronoun them."},{"n":73,"q":"A chart shows that monthly water use fell from 120 units in April to 90 in May. Which statement is accurate?","c":["Water use increased by 30 units.","Water use decreased by 30 units.","Water use remained unchanged.","Water use fell to zero."],"a":1,"why":"120 minus 90 equals a decrease of 30 units."},{"n":74,"q":"Choose the best transition: “The first source describes the policy’s cost. ___, the second explains its effects on families.”","c":["In addition","Instead of","Despite","Unless"],"a":0,"why":"In addition adds a related but different aspect of the topic."},{"n":75,"q":"Which sentence is a fragment?","c":["The committee approved the revised schedule.","Because the buses arrived late.","The buses arrived late, so the meeting began later.","Although the task was difficult, the group finished."],"a":1,"why":"The dependent clause beginning with because does not express a complete thought by itself."},{"n":76,"q":"A museum label says: “The ceramic bowl was made around 900 CE and was found near a coastal settlement.” Which claim is directly supported?","c":["The bowl was made exactly in 900 CE.","The bowl was found near a coastal settlement.","The bowl belonged to a king.","The bowl was used for storing fish."],"a":1,"why":"Only the location of discovery is stated directly; the other claims add unsupported details."},{"n":77,"q":"Choose the best revision: “The reason the trail closed was because of heavy rain.”","c":["The reason the trail closed was due to the fact of heavy rain.","The trail closed because of heavy rain.","The trail closed, the reason was heavy rain.","Because heavy rain. The trail closed."],"a":1,"why":"This revision removes redundancy and states the cause clearly."},{"n":78,"q":"A writer claims that a new bus route improved access. Which evidence is most relevant?","c":["A photo of the bus station","Ridership and travel-time data before and after the route began","A quote about the color of the buses","A list of unrelated road names"],"a":1,"why":"Before-and-after ridership and travel-time data directly address access."},{"n":79,"q":"Choose the correct agreement: “Neither the map nor the directions ___ easy to follow.”","c":["is","was","are","has been"],"a":2,"why":"The nearer subject, directions, is plural, so the verb is plural."},{"n":80,"q":"A paragraph states: “The device costs more initially, but it uses less electricity each month.” Which information would best help evaluate its long-term value?","c":["Its color options","Its expected lifespan and electricity savings over time","The date it was advertised","The number of stores selling it"],"a":1,"why":"Long-term cost depends on lifespan and cumulative energy savings."},{"n":81,"q":"Choose the sentence with correct parallel structure.","c":["The course asks students to read carefully, writing clearly, and revision.","The course asks students reading carefully, to write clearly, and revise.","The course asks students to read carefully, write clearly, and revise thoughtfully.","The course asks students to read carefully, clear writing, and revising."],"a":2,"why":"The three coordinated verbs use parallel infinitive forms."},{"n":82,"q":"A short article notes that a neighborhood added benches and shade near a bus stop. Which purpose is most likely?","c":["To make waiting more comfortable","To increase the distance to the stop","To prevent buses from stopping","To replace the bus service"],"a":0,"why":"Benches and shade are amenities that improve comfort while waiting."},{"n":83,"q":"Choose the correct word: “The findings are consistent ___ the earlier study.”","c":["to","with","for","by"],"a":1,"why":"The standard expression is consistent with."},{"n":84,"q":"A report says: “Attendance rose after the school introduced free breakfast. The report did not examine other changes during that period.” Which caution is most appropriate?","c":["The breakfast program definitely caused the increase.","The increase may be related to the program, but other factors were not ruled out.","Attendance did not rise.","The report proves breakfast has no effect."],"a":1,"why":"The report shows a relationship in time but does not establish causation."},{"n":85,"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","c":["The team tested three materials, wood, metal, and plastic.","The team tested three materials: wood, metal, and plastic.","The team tested: three materials wood, metal, and plastic.","The team tested three materials; wood, metal, and plastic."],"a":1,"why":"A colon correctly introduces the list after a complete clause. EST Session 19 | Diagnostic Mock Test Page 14"}]}]}]}});})();
